      "id": "bbc-2026-09-02-woman-charged-with-juror-intimidation-in-lindsay-clancy-tria",
      "title": "Woman charged with juror intimidation in Lindsay Clancy trial",
      "summary": "Dawn Light is accused of taking photos of jurors outside the Plymouth courthouse, a practice prohibited by the court.",
      "body": "Figure caption, Dawn Light was chased by a scrum of reporters as she left courtByAna FaguyReporting from court in MassachusettsA woman, who was arrested for taking photos of jury members outside the courthouse in the Lindsay Clancy trial, has been charged with aggravated intimidation of a juror. Dawn Light, 56, is accused of sitting in a restricted area outside the Plymouth courthouse and photographing jurors as they exited the building on Tuesday. Her lawyer called it a misunderstanding as she pleaded not guilty to the charges.Lindsay Clancy, 36, is charged with the murders of her three children. The livestreamed trial has captured national and international media attention, and drawn strong, often deeply divided reactions from audiences.The jury has been deliberating on the case since Thursday.During Light's appearance in the court on Wednesday, Massachusetts lawyers said the sanctity of the judicial system was at risk because of her actions.Light, a registered nurse with no prior criminal record, was ordered to stay away from the courthouse grounds and also from those associated with the Clancy trial. She was also ordered not to produce or disseminate photos that could have been preserved on iCloud.Prosecutors requested a $50,000 (£37,000) cash bail, which was denied by the judge. \"This is a complete misunderstanding, Your Honour. My client was trying to get a picture of Ms Clancy,\" Light's attorney Jennifer White said in court. After the arraignment, a swarm of reporters gathered around Light. When one asked, \"Why did you want to see Lindsay [Clancy]\", Light replied, \"Why not?\".Authorities said they found Light in a restricted area in her parked car on Tuesday. When asked if she was taking videos, she said she was not and explained that she was waiting for Clancy.Prosecutors alleged that when they went through Light's deleted folder on her phone, they found photos of jurors exiting the courthouse, and Light was then taken into custody.Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Dawn Light outside the Plymouth courthouseProsecutors said the courthouse has taken extensive measures to protect the trial that has created a spectacle in the coastal Massachusetts town. They described Light's conduct as a serious matter. Judge William Sullivan, who is overseeing the Clancy trial, has an order specifically prohibiting the photographing or filming of jurors.There are more than 200 reporters covering the case, and a group of women supporters - ebbing and flowing in size - have been coming to the courthouse over the past few weeks. The case has also  drawn wide attention on social media, stoking broader debates around mental health, postpartum psychosis, and the limits of criminal responsibility.The Plymouth, Massachusetts, jury has been deliberating since Thursday over whether Clancy is criminally responsible for killing her children.Despite the enormous media footprint, jurors deliberating the case are barred from watching or reading coverage of the trial. They are tasked with agreeing on a verdict solely on the evidence and testimony shown in court.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-02T17:05:09.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/0826/live/c1835830-a6e8-11f1-9e4c-79c1c0e340c6.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwyze4593zwo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-02-range-rover-s-new-ev-looks-just-like-a-regular-range-rover-t",
      "title": "Range Rover’s new EV looks just like a regular Range Rover — that’s refreshing",
      "summary": "I think automakers are finally getting the memo. Rather than try to reinvent the wheel with its new electric vehicle, Range Rover just made one that looks nearly identical to its lineup of gas-powered SUVs. They didn't try to pass off some sleek, spaceship-looking pod as a Range Rover. Sometimes familiar designs are the way to go. \nOf course, under the surface the Range Rover Electric is nothing l",
      "body": "I think automakers are finally getting the memo. Rather than try to reinvent the wheel with its new electric vehicle, Range Rover just made one that looks nearly identical to its lineup of gas-powered SUVs. They didn’t try to pass off some sleek, spaceship-looking pod as a Range Rover. Sometimes familiar designs are the way to go.\nOf course, under the surface the Range Rover Electric is nothing like its internal combustion engine sporting brethren. Twin permanent magnet 260kW electric motors are powered by a double-stacked 118.5 kWh lithium-ion battery, with an estimated range of 330 real-world miles.\nThe all-wheel drive system puts out 542 horsepower and 627 lb.-ft. of torque, which Range Rover describes as “V8 levels of power.”&nbsp;An 800-volt architecture enables DC fast charging from 10–80 percent in just 22 minutes, or about 137 miles in 10 minutes, when plugged into a 350 kW charger. A zero to 60 mph time of 4.3 seconds won’t win it any awards, especially compared to EV rivals like Lucid and Rivian. But this Range Rover probably doesn’t need to blow up any records to make its mark. With a familiar design and decent enough specs, it should be more than sufficient for anyone who has been pining for a touch of British sophistication in their EV shopping.\nThe wait has certainly stretched on longer than it should have. Range Rover first announced it was working on an electric model over five years ago. It first opened preorders in 2023, promising that customer deliveries would begin a year later. The Jaguar Land Rover–owned brand took its sweet time fulfilling these promises, but now that the cover has been lifted, the automaker is taking orders at a starting price of £154,070 in the UK (or about $140,450&nbsp;in the US).\nJLR says it will produce the Range Rover Electric at its Solihull factory, alongside gas and hybrid models. It’s also touting the number of patents submitted for this EV —&nbsp;300 — making it the most technologically custom-built Range Rover ever.\nHow technologically advanced? Try lightning-fast grip control. Instead of waiting for mechanical gears to adjust when tires slip, the electric motors adjust power in 50 milliseconds — 100 times faster than a gas car. This gives instant control on ice, mud, or loose gravel. The one-pedal drive system is tuned for super steep slopes of up to 45 degrees, so you can climb mountains or crawl over obstacles using just the accelerator. And the battery and motors are fully sealed, allowing you to drive through nearly 3 feet of water (900mm) without damaging the vehicle. Lastly, a built-in heat pump reuses waste heat to warm the cabin and keep the battery at the right temperature, preserving up to 25 extra miles of driving range in freezing weather.\nAll of this sounds great on paper. Stacked up against rivals like the Porsche Macan EV or the Lotus Eletre, the SUV may look even better — especially for those car buyers who still prefer a familiar face on their electric vehicle.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Cars",
        "Electric Cars",
        "Jaguar",
        "News",
        "Transportation"
      ],
      "author": "Andrew J. Hawkins",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-02T17:01:08.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/RANGE-ROVER-ELECTRIC-FRONT-3-4-02_09_26_1.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=4.752148272957,0,90.495703454086,100",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/transportation/988232/range-rover-electric-range-battery-specs",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-02-south-african-airline-behind-daring-stadium-stunt-cancels-ne",
      "title": "South African airline behind daring  stadium stunt cancels next flyby, regulator says",
      "summary": "The civil aviation authority says it is also investigating last Saturday's aerial display.",
      "body": "The South African airline behind a daring aerial stunt last Saturday that saw two planes flying in close formation low over a packed stadium has pulled out of doing a similar operation this weekend, the country's aviation regulator, SACAA, has said.Airlink's flying feat divided opinion with some labelling the startling manoeuvre reckless, while others praised the precision and expertise on display.SACAA said that while it had approved the applications and safety measures for the flybys, it was now investigating further following \"discussions in the media\".Airlink has not commented on the reported cancellation of Saturday's display that had been due to place over a stadium in Soweto.Footage of last weekend's air show before the start of a rugby match in Cape Town, featuring fierce rivals South Africa and New Zealand, was shared around the world.The flight tracking website Flightradar24 said that according to its analysis \"the lead aircraft passed less than 50ft (15m) above the top of the stadium\".From some angles the jets appeared very close to the roof of the DHL Stadium as they roared over amid coloured flare smoke.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-02T16:58:59.000Z",
