    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-02-google-dodges-another-breakup-attempt",
      "title": "Google dodges another breakup attempt",
      "summary": "US District Court Judge Leonie Brinkema declined the Justice Department's request to make Google sell off parts of its ad tech business, accepting milder remedies to restore competition to markets it illegally monopolized for years.\nBrinkema said she would adopt most of the behavioral changes proposed by the parties, with some modifications. But those won't become public until the parties meet to ",
      "body": "US District Court Judge Leonie Brinkema declined the Justice Department’s request to make Google sell off parts of its ad tech business, accepting milder remedies to restore competition to markets it illegally monopolized for years.\nBrinkema said she would adopt most of the behavioral changes proposed by the parties, with some modifications. But those won’t become public until the parties meet to work out any additional proposed revisions and to review the judge’s opinion for confidential information that must be redacted. They could include proposals like restricting Google from using self-preferencing ad auction tactics or letting third-party ad tech tools access the same real-time information as Google.&nbsp;\nOnce the final opinion comes out, Google can decide to appeal Brinkema’s underlying decision ruling it an illegal ad tech monopoly, as it recently did for a separate judgement that it had monopolized the online search market. In that case, Judge Amit Mehta also declined to go as far as a breakup, opting instead for Google to share data with competitors and alter its behavior.&nbsp;\nIn the ad tech case, the DOJ successfully argued that Google had illegally monopolized the markets for publisher ad servers — where publishers can manage ad space for sale on their sites — and ad exchanges, which facilitate the auctions through which that ad space is sold. The judge agreed that Google had illegally tied its publisher ad server, Doubleclick for Publishers (DFP), and AdX ad exchange together in an anticompetitive manner that made it nearly impossible for customers to leave, degrading any competition. However, Brinkema found that the DOJ failed to prove Google illegally monopolized a market for advertiser-side tools. Ryan Pauley, president of The Verge’s parent company PMX Global, was deposed during the case.\n“We’re very pleased the Court rejected the DOJ’s proposal to break apart tools that help small businesses reach new customers and grow,” Google VP of regulatory affairs Lee-Anne Mulholland said in a statement. In a statement on X, the DOJ Antitrust Division said it was “pleased that the court ordered substantial relief” and that it was “evaluating appropriate next steps.” DOJ Associate AG Stanley Woodward Jr. added, “The timing of the Court’s order reflects the tradeoffs between immediate relief and remedies obtained through years of litigation.”\nGoogle has been the subject of antitrust backlash under both Democratic and Republican administrations. During the Biden administration, then-Sen. JD Vance (R-OH) said Federal Trade Commission Chair Lina Khan was “one of the few people in the Biden administration who I think is doing a pretty good job,” largely as a result of her scrutiny on tech.\nThe remedies decision concludes the district court phase of the third major tech monopoly case brought by the federal government in recent years. Besides the two Google antitrust cases brought by the DOJ, the Federal Trade Commission brought a monopolization case against Meta, which it recently lost, and trials between federal enforcers and Amazon and Apple, respectively, are expected to take place in the coming years.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Antitrust",
        "Google",
        "Policy",
        "Politics",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Lauren Feiner",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-02T15:42:17.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/03/236780_Google_AntiTrust_Trial_Custom_Art_CVirginia__0000_4.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/policy/988316/google-ad-tech-antitrust-remedies-decision",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-02-four-in-court-over-murder-of-baby-stabbed-to-death",
      "title": "Four in court over murder of baby stabbed to death",
      "summary": "Four people, aged between 19 and 38, appear in court charged with murdering the newborn.",
      "body": "Two men and two women have appeared in court charged with murdering a baby girl who had been repeatedly stabbed at a house in Sheffield.The newborn - referred to as Baby S in court - was found at a property in Holywell Heights, in the Wincobank area, on Sunday, having suffered 33 wounds from 15 separate stabbings.Andrea Skopova, 20, formerly of Robey Street, Sheffield, and Peter Horvath, 19, Nina Horvathova, 37, and Peter Horvath, 38, all of Holywell Heights, appeared at Sheffield Magistrates' Court.The four Slovakian nationals have been charged with murder, causing or allowing the death of a child and perverting the course of justice by taking steps to conceal the killing of an infant.A fifth person, a 15-year-old girl, who cannot be named for legal reasons, also appeared in the same court charged with perverting the course of justice.All five defendants were remanded into custody to appear at Sheffield Crown Court on Thursday. Tuesday's hearing was told the baby's body was found on top of a washing machine in an outhouse and was wrapped in a towel inside a blue bag.Prosecutor Luke Hopkinson said the defendants had been accused of perverting the course of justice by cleaning the scene.Earlier, police said two women, aged 26 and 37, who were arrested on suspicion of murder remained on bail.Speaking ahead of the court hearing, Det Ch Insp Tom Woodward said: \"This is a significant step in our investigation into the tragic circumstances that resulted in the death of this baby girl.\"I would like to thank our communities, who I know will have been deeply impacted by this traumatic incident, for supporting us as we work through this intricate investigation.\"We would ask the public to avoid speculating and posting information online about this case while the legal process takes it course.\"Protecting the integrity of the criminal justice process is crucial to us securing justice for this little girl.\"Police said officers would continue to be in and around the Holywell Heights area carrying out inquiries. Listen to highlights from South Yorkshire on BBC Sounds, catch up with the latest episode of Look North",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-02T15:35:13.000Z",
