      "id": "bbc-2026-09-02-range-rover-launches-first-fully-electric-model",
      "title": "Range Rover launches first fully electric model",
      "summary": "The vehicle is being built at JLR's manufacturing plant in Solihull, in the West Midlands.",
      "body": "Image source, Jaguar Land RoverImage caption, The prototype's launch comes after owner JLR reported falling profits and supply‑chain disruptionByShannen HeadleyWest MidlandsRange Rover has unveiled its first fully electric model, after a year‑long delay to its planned launch.The Range Rover Electric, to be built in Solihull, marks a major shift in its electrification programme, backed by large‑scale upgrades across its West Midlands sites.Parent company JLR said it had up-skilled 10,500 workers as it pushes ahead with modernisation despite recent financial pressures and planned job cuts.The launch comes as the Coventry‑based carmaker faces falling profits, supply chain disruption and restructuring following a cyber attack a year ago that cost an estimated £1.9bn, making it the most economically damaging cyber event in UK history, researchers said. JLR's electric roll-out comes after the turbulence continued into 2026, with profits dropping to £66m in the first quarter after a fire at a key Norwegian supplier. The company said the launch was being supported by new battery and electric drive unit production lines, as well as training about 9,000 employees in Solihull for electrification and a further 1,500 across the West Midlands region.Image source, Jaguar Land Rover/PAImage caption, The launch of the all-electric model comes in the wake of an announcement by JLR in July that hundreds of jobs were to be cut JLR's electric propulsion manufacturing centre, in Wolverhampton, is now producing battery packs and electric drive units alongside internal combustion engines.Martin Limpert, managing director of Range Rover, said the new model was the result of a decade of \"considered engineering\". \"This is a pivotal for us at Range Rover, for JLR, I really believe that for us it's also for our partners for our retailers for our communities,\" he told the BBC.\"This secures jobs this provides for the economy and we are really excited.\"'A profound change'David Bailey, professor of business economics at the University of Birmingham, said the launch was the \"start of a new era\" for the firm.\"It's hugely important, as it's the start of their proper shift into electrification and its going to mean a profound change for the company but also the supply chain in the West Midlands.\" he said.The business is also undergoing restructuring, confirming that fewer than 300 roles will go less than a year after a cyber attack halted production for more than a month.At the time of the results, the firm said new product launches would leave \"JLR in good shape whilst acknowledging the continuing geopolitical, inflationary and regulatory challenges the industry faces\".Get in touchTell us which stories we should cover in Birmingham and the Black CountryMore on this storyRelated internet links",
