    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-02-ask-hn-how-do-you-survive-a-job-you-hate-which-is-slowly-mak",
      "title": "Ask HN: How do you survive a job you hate which is slowly making you sick?",
      "summary": "I started a new job a little less than a year ago as a product owner/manager for a legacy enterprise system. Unfortunately the job has not at all been what I expected and what was sold to me in the interview process for a number of reasons.\nI don't want to get too much into it, but the main reason is that my job was supposed to be leading a modernization project to expand our legacy enterprise sys",
      "body": "Ask HN: How do you survive a job you hate which is slowly making you sick?36 points by _override 2 hours ago  | hide | past | favorite | 45 commentsI started a new job a little less than a year ago as a product owner/manager for a legacy enterprise system. Unfortunately the job has not at all been what I expected and what was sold to me in the interview process for a number of reasons.I don't want to get too much into it, but the main reason is that my job was supposed to be leading a modernization project to expand our legacy enterprise system with modern API-based integrations, but our development team simply does not have experience with building, testing, shipping, and maintaining APIs, which has made my job as a PO/PM considerably more difficult. Things I was used to taking a week or two max at other companies take many months to ship and aren't really built with the quality I would expect. This makes it really awkward for me when we have to meet with customers and mention that modernization and system integrations are our top priority according to the management team, yet we can't even ship a few GET endpoints after six months of work...I could go on and on, but honestly I just strongly dislike this job. I'm trying my best to find another role, but the market is really competitive right now where I live (Sweden). I would say I have a quite decent CV, with a masters from an excellent university and around a decade of work experience in tech, but Sweden currently has the third highest unemployment rate in the EU so there is a huge amount of competition for each role. But of course, all I can do is do my best.My main concern is that this job is slowly making me sick. I have started to have really concerning mental health symptoms, specifically severe anxiety, due to this job. I've also had some really dark thoughts which has scared me a lot. My doctor started me on some antidepressants and I am receiving therapy, but it's too early to say if it has helped or not.I don't know how much more I can take before I end up on some kind of sick leave. But I'm trying to take it day by day and I am trying to stay positive. Hopefully I can find another job soon, but until then I am stuck in this job.For those of you who have been stuck in jobs you strongly dislike, how did you manage to separate your work from your life so that you could survive the situation? Did you find something that worked for you to ease the situation until you could find another job?Thanks for taking the time to read this. I really appreciate any feedback or advice. help",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "_override",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-02T07:43:49.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49533073",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-02-dell-s-newest-laptop-sounds-a-lot-like-a-macbook-neo",
      "title": "Dell’s newest laptop sounds a lot like a MacBook Neo",
      "summary": "That’s a bold color, especially for a Dell. | Image: Dell\t\n\nDell's new Dell 14S laptop sounds a lot like a MacBook Neo: it comes in four colors, is aimed at students, and it starts with 8GB of RAM and 256GB of storage. Announced at IFA 2026, the 14S is meant to sit below the recently launched XPS 13 and offer another affordable laptop option as most other models continue to climb in price. The 14S",
      "body": "Dell’s new Dell 14S laptop sounds a lot like a MacBook Neo: it comes in four colors, is aimed at students, and it starts with 8GB of RAM and 256GB of storage. Announced at IFA 2026, the 14S is meant to sit below the recently launched XPS 13 and offer another affordable laptop option as most other models continue to climb in price. The 14S will come in linen (beige), dusty rose (pink), washed denim (blue), and velvet green (yep, that’s green). That’s much more colorful than the XPS 13, and like its fancier brethren, the 14S can be configured with 16GB of RAM and a 512GB or 1TB SSD.\nThe Dell 14S isn’t as thin or as light as the XPS 13, but in addition to the same two USB-C 3.2 Gen 2 ports with DisplayPort and Power Delivery, it also has HDMI 1.4 and a 3.5mm audio jack. (Why the XPS 13 lacks a headphone jack, I’ll never understand). But the 14S has much more basic, non-touch display options: a 300-nit 1920 x 1200 / 60Hz LCD or a 450-nit 2880 x 1800 / 120Hz one.&nbsp;\nThere are some other niceties for students on the 14S, including a 1080p webcam with Windows Hello face unlock support, and battery life that Dell claims can last for up to 23 hours of Netflix streaming. For chip offerings, it’ll be either the Intel Core 5 320 or Core 7 350 — the 14S won’t scale up to Intel Panther Lake options like the XPS 13 is soon expected to.\nWe don’t know how much any of this will cost just yet, as pricing is TBD for now, but in a virtual briefing Dell reps said the 14S will be close to the XPS 13’s $700 starting price yet slightly more accessible. We’ll know actual prices closer to its release later this fall. I’m currently testing and reviewing the XPS 13, and I’ve been very impressed with the hardware (except for the missing headphone jack). I’m all for seeing more good, affordable laptops out there —&nbsp;hopefully the 14S makes the right compromises to get the price down.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Dell",
        "Gadgets",
        "IFA 2026",
        "Laptops",
        "News",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Antonio G. Di Benedetto",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-02T07:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/Dell-14S_Velvet-Green_Lifestyle.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,16.709105790342,100,66.581788419316",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/gadgets/987839/dell-14s-student-laptop-ifa-2026-price-specs",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-02-five-moments-that-shaped-taylor-s-legendary-career",
      "title": "Five moments that shaped Taylor's legendary career",
      "summary": "From writing a letter to her idol as a 10-year-old to messaging a promoter on social media, here are five sliding doors moments that helped shape Katie Taylor’s career.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Katie Taylor will fight for the final time after compiling a 25-1 professional record, becoming an undisputed world champion at two weights and winning Olympic gold and five world amateur titlesByKal SajadBBC Sport boxing journalist in DublinOn Saturday, Katie Taylor steps through the ropes at an 82,000-capacity Croke Park to face France's Flora Pili in her farewell fight.Victory on the hallowed Dublin turf would write one final fairytale chapter - a chance to bow out as undisputed light-welterweight champion and put the seal on a career that has revolutionised women's sport.There have been countless moments that have shaped the 40-year-old's journey.From a childhood letter to a Twitter direct message, these are five sliding doors moments in the making of an Irish icon.The letter to Deirdre GogartyImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Ireland's first female world boxing champion Deirdre Gogarty formed a lasting relationship with Katie Taylor after the aspiring 10-year-old boxer wrote her a letterLong before Taylor became Olympic champion, she was a 10-year-old girl in Bray who simply wanted to box.There was nowhere for her to go though. Women's boxing was unsanctioned in Ireland, absent from the Olympic programme and devoid of any clear professional pathway.But there was Deirdre Gogarty. Having relocated to the United States, she became Ireland's first female professional world champion in 1997 when she defeated Bonnie Canino for the WIBF featherweight title in Louisiana.\"How did you do it when everyone tried to stop you?\" a 10-year-old Taylor asked in a letter sent to her hero.\"If you keep working at it and keep showing your skills, somebody's going to say 'we've got to let this girl box',\" Gogarty replied.Reflecting on the letter years later, Gogarty added: \"It's mind-blowing. Especially when she wrote, 'maybe one day they'll let us box in the Olympics'.\"Had that letter never been written, Taylor might still have found her way to the top, but Gogarty offered hope when none seemingly existed.Heartbreak at Rio 2016 and the leap into the unknownImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Four years after winning gold at the London Olympics, Katie Taylor lost to Finland's Mira Potkonen in the Rio 2016 quarter-finalsWhen the International Olympic Committee was undecided on including women's boxing at the Games, Taylor was brought in for showcase events in front of sceptical officials in Chicago. Her dazzling displays dismantled every argument.\"Katie won't say this, and I've been holding back from saying this, but single-handedly, Katie Taylor got women's boxing into the Olympics,\" her manager Brian Peters says.Women's boxing made its Olympic debut at the London 2012 Games and Taylor claimed lightweight gold.Four years later in Rio, however, that amateur journey ended when a split-decision quarter-final defeat shattered Taylor's title defence. She was at a crossroads - rebuild for another Olympic cycle or turn professional in an era where women's pro boxing was still neglected.\"It was probably the worst year of my career,\" Taylor later admitted. \"I needed a change. I needed a new mindset.\"Had she secured a second Olympic gold, the motivation to dive into the pro ranks might have evaporated. Instead, the sting of defeat forced reinvention.Hearn's DMs & Taylor's loyaltyImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Katie Taylor has remained with manager Brian Peters (left), trainer Ross Enamait (middle) and promoter Eddie Hearn (right) throughout her professional careerTurning professional was one thing, but securing both the right team and commercial backing was another challenge.First came manager Peters, who initially stepped in out of concern for her future.\"I only took her on not to see her end up with people who didn't know what they were doing,\" Peters says.On 4 October 2016, Taylor sent Matchroom's Eddie Hearn a direct message on Twitter, now known as X, asking if he would consider promoting her. Hearn replied 24 hours later, inviting Taylor, Peters, and her mother Bridget to a London restaurant. Hearn even borrowed his father's car to impress Taylor.The charm offensive worked and Taylor made her debut at Wembley Arena a month later.In a sport notorious for fractured relationships and bitter splits, Taylor's core team has remained the same from day one with Peters, Hearn and trainer Ross Enamait.\"In a business full of egos, she's stayed loyal to everyone around her and remained the most humble person you could ever meet,\" Peters says.Had Hearn dismissed that DM - or had Taylor lacked the enduring loyalty that kept the same tight-knit team in her corner for a decade - the promotional engine that transformed women's boxing might never have started.Image caption, Eddie Hearn shared the direct message he received from Katie Taylor in October 2016MSG & the Serrano testImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Taylor fought Amanda Serrano three times in the United States, twice at Madison Square Garden, in arguably the greatest rivalry in women's boxing historyBy April 2022, Taylor was the undisputed lightweight champion. She had already survived a razor-thin majority decision against Delfine Persoon at Madison Square Garden (MSG) three years earlier to unify - a night when many felt her aura of invincibility was almost cracked.Returning to MSG to face seven-division title winner Amanda Serrano presented a different magnitude of threat.Their blockbuster marked the first time two women headlined the storied New York venue. In front of a sellout crowd of 19,187, Taylor claimed a split-decision victory in what was widely hailed as the fight of the year.Had one scorecard leaned the other way or had the bout failed to deliver on its enormous hype, the momentum behind women's mega-fights would have stalled.Instead, the victory spawned a record-breaking rivalry. Their 2024 rematch drew global viewing figures on Netflix on the undercard of Jake Paul v Mike Tyson, before Taylor sealed the trilogy at the Garden in 2025.Taylor v PiliSaturday, 5 SeptemberCroke Park, DublinCoverage on Radio 5 Sports Extra from 19:00 BST, then 5 Live from 22:00. Ringwalks for main event expected between 22:00 and 22:30.Cameron II: Legacy on the lineImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Taylor avenged the first defeat of her professional career by beating Chantelle Cameron in their 2023 rematch in Dublin to become undisputed super-lightweight world championTaylor's first professional defeat came in May 2023 at Dublin's 3Arena when she was outpointed by Chantelle Cameron for the undisputed light-welterweight belts.At 36, questions around retirement surfaced.In the ring immediately following the defeat, Peters says he told his fighter to congratulate Cameron and ask for the rematch.Six months later, Taylor delivered a masterclass in grit, claiming a majority decision to become a two-weight undisputed champion.\"The pressure was immense,\" Peters says.\"For all she had done, her legacy was suddenly questioned. Had Chantelle won the rematch, who knows what would have happened?\"The final pay-off - Croke ParkImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Fighting at Croke Park has long been a dream for Taylor, who has described the prospect of appearing at the iconic Dublin stadium as the pinnacle of her professional careerCroke Park is not really a sixth sliding door but rather the final pay-off.After beating Serrano in last year's trilogy decider, Taylor quietly decided she was done.\"She actually retired that night,\" Peters says. \"She said, 'We convincingly beat Serrano - where do we go from there?' I said I'll agree on one condition.\"That condition was Croke Park or nothing.\"Croke Park - no disrespect to Wembley - is the greatest stadium in the world,\" Peters says. \"You cut an Irish person and they'll bleed Croke Park.\"Peters insists there will be no change of heart - no Cameron trilogy and no comebacks.\"When Katie gives her word, that is it,\" he says.Regardless of Saturday's result, Taylor will be remembered as a pioneer who kicked open the door for women's boxing.More boxing from the BBC",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
