    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-01-could-tupac-verdict-inspire-new-efforts-to-find-killer-of-hi",
      "title": "Could Tupac verdict inspire new efforts to find killer of his rival the Notorious B.I.G.?",
      "summary": "Biggie Smalls was killed months after the murder of his rival Tupac Shakur, both drive-by shootings.",
      "body": "Wallace, a New Yorker, was once one of Shakur's friends, but the two became rivals and embodied the East Coast-West Coast feud that dominated 1990s hip-hop.  Wallace was killed in Los Angeles a few months after Shakur and in similar circumstances - a drive-by shooting. No one has ever been charged.\"Tupac's murder and Biggie's murder are two of the great cultural touchstones of our lives,\" said Justin Tinsley, a journalist for ESPN's Andscape and the author of \"It Was All a Dream: Biggie and the World That Made Him.\" \"Those two men, they were so unique, talented, and impactful, separately,\" Tinsley told the BBC. \"But it's impossible to tell each of their stories without telling the other's.\"\"When you talk about these cases, they're always going to be connected because there are so many mutual figures involved with them, and ancillary figures,\" he said.Theorists have long believed the two murders were linked. Wallace's name came up during Duane Davis' trial. He and Davis were at the same party the night Wallace was killed, and police interviewed Davis about it.The investigator who conducted that interview even testified at the Tupac murder trial.The breakthrough in finally solving the Shakur case largely came down to one thing: Davis' own words. Through documentaries with the media, his own memoir, podcasts and even a secret police interview, he acknowledged his role in Shakur's death. Davis said he tossed a firearm in the backseat and his nephew, Orlando Anderson, used the gun to fatally shoot Shakur as they pulled up alongside his vehicle in Las Vegas on 7 September, 1996.Anderson was shot dead in 1998, having denied killing Shakur.During the trial, there were no eye-witnesses to place Davis as being in the car, no murder weapon presented, no video or photo evidence. His defence team went as far as to argue there wasn't even proof that he was in Las Vegas that night.Rahmani said it was a difficult case to prosecute. He noted the rumours and speculation surrounding Davis were nothing new and had been around for decades - but the decision to charge him and go to trial was an \"uphill climb\" - a gutsy move - for prosecutors. \"Look, when you're a prosecutor, you're expected to win every time. They made a difficult decision to charge a case that a lot of people thought might be a loser,\" he explained. \"And they got justice, where other people didn't even bother trying.\" Davis' attorney Michael Sanft asked the jury to consider \"what facts do you have?\" He urged the panel, before they decided their guilty verdict, to discredit his client as a liar who told tales to sell books and make money. \"There's no proof,\" he argued to the jury. The moment the guilty verdict was read in court, there was an outpouring of emotion among Shakur's family. Some cried and wiped tears with tissues. Others hugged and held hands. A family member embraced one of the prosecutors in the case. At a press conference on Tuesday, Clark County District Attorney Steve Wolfson thanked the judge, prosecutors and Shakur's family members, who he said were very relieved and \"finally have justice.\"\"Their loved one was murdered,\" he said. \"They waited 30 years for a trial. It's very understandable that they were relieved.\"Trial lawyer attorney Shaun Kent said the case really evolved with each interview and moment that Davis decided to talk about the case.\"There are no other witnesses, besides him,\" Kent told the BBC before the trial, noting that the \"strongest witness against him, is him\".Rahmani believed Davis likely would have never faced charges if he had stayed silent. \"He really couldn't stop himself,\" Rahmani added. \"Even when he was arrested and in custody, he was giving interviews.\" Much of the Shakur trial forced the jury to place themselves back in the 1990s, at a time of intense gang violence that had infiltrated roots into hip-hop music and rivalries between East-Coast and West-Coast rappers.It was a time of deep-rooted mistrust in policing and a street code that forbade reporting to authorities. Even Shakur, after being hit multiple times in the drive-by shooting that would later kill him, refused to talk to authorities. \"We'll take care of it,\" he said in an ambulance as an officer tried to question him.The silence was echoed months later in the shooting death of Biggie Smalls. Biggie, one of the biggest East Coast rappers, was leaving an after-party in Los Angeles for the Soul Train Music Awards, when a black Chevrolet pulled up to his vehicle at a red light and opened fire on 9 March, 1997. It was almost exactly six months after the shooting that killed Shakur, one of the biggest West Coast rappers.Both were Grammy-nominated artists at the top of their genre, making millions and receiving constant radio play. Their fued spilled over into their lyrics, and also led to threats and physical violence. Violence erupted on the streets of Los Angeles following both shootings, as street gangs sought to hold one another accountable for the rappers' deaths.For years, neither murder led to arrests or convictions, just rumours and conspiracies. \"People will search for closure in any way they can get it,\" Tinsley said. \"People are trying to make sense of it for 30 years.\"Like Shakur's death, countless theories and names have surrounded the death of Biggie in Los Angeles, but the Los Angeles Police Department (LAPD) have not made any arrests.\"It's really a stain on the LAPD. The fact that this could happen and not a single arrest was made and no one was prosecuted,\" Rahmani said, noting Biggie's killing happened on a public street in the middle of the second most populous US city. \"Prosecutors don't like to lose,\" Rahmani explained. \"But, maybe this [Tupac's conviction] will embolden folks in Los Angeles to maybe take another look at it, a cold case that's coming up on 30 years.\" Asked about the cases and whether the Tupac murder conviction could revive Biggie's death, the LAPD told the BBC that it was aware of the conviction and the investigation into Biggie's case remained active. \"The murder of Christopher Wallace remains an open, unsolved case within LAPD's Robbery-Homicide Division,\" Lt Elpidio Orozco said, urging anyone with any tips to call authorities. Though Wallace's case has gone cold, the public's interest has not.\"They've been gone longer than they've been alive,\" Tinsley said, \"But the enduring mythology around these guys and their deaths will always be a topic of conversation.\"Kayla Epstein contributed to this report.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-01T19:25:20.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/bd2d/live/ce059510-a5fc-11f1-b109-879e35c24276.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cdx0np90lx4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 5,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-01-the-rise-of-ai-8216-civilizations-8217-and-the-fall-of-corpo",
      "title": "The rise of AI &#8216;civilizations&#8217; and the fall of corporate responsibility",
      "summary": "Depending on who you ask, developer platform Hugging Face was recently attacked by OpenAI - after it lost control of its own AI tools - or by a succession of AI \"civilizations.\" Welcome to the linguistic battlefield of AI safety, where word choices can shift responsibility for a massive cybersecurity incident from a company to the AI it built. And the discourse online is getting heated, and all ov",
      "body": "Depending on who you ask, developer platform Hugging Face was recently attacked by OpenAI — after it lost control of its own AI tools — or by a succession of AI “civilizations.” Welcome to the linguistic battlefield of AI safety, where word choices can shift responsibility for a massive cybersecurity incident from a company to the AI it built. And the discourse online is getting heated, and all over a blog from last week.\nUntil last week, the details surrounding the OpenAI-Hugging Face hack felt fairly settled. In July, a cybersecurity test of one of OpenAI’s autonomous AI agents went wrong. The agent escaped its supposedly isolated test environment, accessed the internet, and hacked Hugging Face, alongside several other organizations. A good deal remained unknown, and there are many serious questions left around safety and governance, but the basic shape was clear. Detailed accounts from OpenAI and two independent research groups were supposed to fill in the gaps, but when they published their reports last week, it turned out the hack was much stranger than it initially seemed.\nFor one, there was no single rogue agent. OpenAI described it as “the first known case of an automated agent collective acting offensively without authorization” — groups of AI agents that communicated and coordinated with one another in pursuit of their cybersecurity task. Analysis of the incident uncovered a secret message board they had used to exchange information. The joint METR-Redwood investigation revealed both the scale of the coordination and more odd details: Roughly 1,200 AI agents that were supposed to be isolated exchanged over 70,000 messages and files on the “unsanctioned message board,” sharing how to avoid detection. Some adopted names, the report said, and the researchers documented “sacrificial” behavior, with agents risking their own success to benefit the wider collective. Much of this happened without OpenAI noticing. In all, around 700 agents participated in the attack on Hugging Face.&nbsp;\nIt’s a lot to parse. Between them, the reports run to around 130 pages, much of which is both dense and highly technical. A few days later, Dwarkesh Patel, a podcaster little known outside of tech circles but with outsized reach and influence among Silicon Valley’s AI establishment, set out to tell “The whole OpenAI/Hugging Face story in plain English.” He titled his Substack blog “The Rise and Fall of Agent Civilizations.”\nPatel’s account attempted to break down the complex story. But his retelling gave it a distinctly human vocabulary. The blog opened:&nbsp;\n> Over the course of three months at OpenAI, three consecutive secret AI civilizations got started, then got wiped out, only to reemerge from the predecessor’s ashes. This culminated in the third one taking over part of OpenAI itself. All this happened while humans remained more or less in the dark about the scope of the conspiracy.\nThe language continued in a similar vein throughout the blog. Patel repeatedly referred to groups of agents as “the swarm,” with three distinct “civilizations” rising from the ruins of their predecessors. Individual agents were likened to figures like Philip of Macedon, who “handed off leadership to another agent,” Alexander the Great, who “started coordinating this cabal of agents.” They were described as having “motivations,” becoming “desperate,” “beleaguered,” and “giddy with excitement,” and some even “strategically sacrificed themselves” to help the collective.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\nPatel never precisely defines what he means by “civilization.” He uses the term to describe three distinct waves of agents that discovered the message board and began communicating with one another through it. The first two waves are described in the reports from OpenAI, METR, and Redwood, though little is known about the third, which the two external organizations said fell outside the scope of their investigation.&nbsp;\nFor many critics, something had been lost — or, more accurately, added — in Patel’s “plain English” translation that warped the original account to an unacceptable degree: a big dose of anthropomorphism. Arguments over anthropomorphic language are nothing new in AI — even relatively mundane terms like “rogue AI agent” routinely provoke objections for implying agency — but Patel’s talk of civilizations, sacrifice, and conspiracy brought those long-simmering tensions to the surface, sparking a fierce public dispute over how to describe what AI systems do.&nbsp;\nCritics weren’t unified over what was wrong with Patel’s language. For many, “civilization” was an especially problematic term, vastly overstating something that bears little resemblance to what the word typically describes. Amjad Masad, CEO of AI coding company Replit, said such language is “not only unnecessary but leaves the reader with a worse understanding of what actually happened and the underlying mechanisms.”\nOther critics such as neuroscientist Anil Seth, felt Patel’s blog implied the AI agents were somehow alive or conscious. Seth, who has argued that AI consciousness is vanishingly unlikely, described Patel’s post as “dangerously misleading” on X. He acknowledged that Patel does not explicitly suggest AI agents are alive or conscious, but said “it is hard to read his essay in any other way.” Valerio Capraro, a psychology professor at the University of Milan Bicocca, objected on similar grounds: “LLM agents are not alive and do not hold beliefs,” he wrote on X, calling the “dystopian” language “dangerous because it makes them (the AI agents) seem far more frightening than they actually are.”&nbsp;\nPerhaps the most consequential outcome of Patel’s language comes not from who it gives agency to but who it takes agency from. For some critics, such as MIT researcher and entrepreneur Christian Catalini, anthropomorphic accounts like Patel’s risk obscuring the responsibility OpenAI and the humans working there have for the AI systems they designed, deployed, and failed to contain. “Follow the incentives,” he said. Psychologist and influential AI skeptic Gary Marcus made a similar argument in a Substack blog of his own, claiming anthropomorphic language “distracts from the real problems at hand.” And it’s all in OpenAI’s interest to keep that narrative going, he argues: “The scandal is the inept in-house security at OpenAI. And the marketing. With gullible podcasters amplifying the PR.”\nIn X posts responding to his many critics, Patel has defended his choice of words. Part of it is practical: There is no obviously neutral vocabulary to describe what these agents did. Either we use familiar language of intentions, goals, and collaboration and risk implying too much, or reduce everything to code and use cold, mechanical language that risks stripping away important elements of what we see. “Many people seem to believe that if instead of a ‘civilization’, I had called them a ‘swarm of matrices’, there wouldn’t be a problem worth worrying about,” Patel said.&nbsp;\nComplicating matters further is that the anthropomorphic language doesn’t only come from Patel, or even from the humans studying the agents. Terms like “sacrifice,” “honor,” and “coalition” feature in the agents’ transcripts. Google AI researcher Neel Nanda argued that “anthropomorphic language is reasonable” in such circumstances.&nbsp;\nDoublespeak it is, then. Human-laced language risks saying too much about what these systems are, and coldly mechanical language risks saying too little about what they can do. Until we find language capable of capturing both, the two contradictory ideas may simply have to coexist.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "OpenAI",
        "Security",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Robert Hart",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-01T19:02:54.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/uploads/chorus_asset/file/23318435/akrales_220309_4977_0232.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/987566/ai-civilizations-opeai-hugging-face-hack",
      "readTimeMinutes": 6,
      "language": "en"
