      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-31-microsoft-tests-fix-for-latest-hours-long-outlook-outage",
      "title": "Microsoft tests fix for latest hours-long Outlook outage",
      "summary": "Microsoft says it's testing a fix for the widespread Outlook issues that have led to email delays and failures.",
      "body": "11:56 AM PDT · August 31, 2026\nImage Credits:Jaap Arriens/NurPhoto / Getty Images\nSnow day? Microsoft on Monday confirmed a widespread, multi-hour outage affecting Outlook users, leading to email delays and failures, authentication issues, and other problems.\nIn a post on X, Microsoft reported the issue was impacting its Exchange Online service, its cloud-hosted platform for business email, calendar, contacts, and tasks, and then said it had identified the problem.\n“We’ve confirmed that users may experience degraded functionality with various Exchange Online functionalities. We’re reviewing service telemetry and diagnostic data to isolate the source of the issue,” Microsoft wrote at 12:43 p.m. ET on Monday, after an earlier message saying it was investigating user reports of Exchange Online problems.\nThe company said that it identified an issue with an “authentication component” and is testing what it believes will be a remediation strategy with a portion of the infrastructure to determine if it resolves the problem. If so, it will roll out the solution more broadly. Later, it noted that the authentication component was impacting other services beyond Exchange.\nWe've identified an issue with an authentication component which is contributing to impact. To address this, we've developed a remediation strategy and we're applying it to a portion of infrastructure to test its efficacy. Once we've confirmed this resolves impact, we'll apply…— Microsoft 365 Status (@MSFT365Status) August 31, 2026\nWe're continuing to fine-tune our remediation strategy as our internal tests progress. Additionally, we've confirmed that the authentication component issue impacts other services beyond Exchange Online. For more information regarding other impact scenarios, please see:…— Microsoft 365 Status (@MSFT365Status) August 31, 2026\nThe outage is another reminder of how much of the business world today still runs on email, and particularly Outlook and Exchange.\nThis has been a fairly lengthy outage, as user reports started streaming in around 11:30 a.m. ET on Monday, according to the crowdsourced outage detection website Downdetector. By 2 p.m. ET, the site was seeing north of 5,000 user reports. Meanwhile, many others were complaining directly on social media platforms, like X.\nThose impacted by the outage may see delays and failures in sending or receiving messages, failures when trying to search for content in their mailbox, authentication errors, failures with mailbox operations, and other workflows related to message delivery.\nAs of 5 PM ET on Monday, Microsoft was still trying to resolve the problem.\nOur investigation indicates that a misconfiguration issue may be preventing authentication components from deploying as expected to a portion of infrastructure, and we're reexamining recent changes made to the service to determine why this is occurring. Additionally, we are…— Microsoft 365 Status (@MSFT365Status) August 31, 2026\nThis article was updated after publication as Microsoft posted more information about the status of the outage.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Sarah Perez",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-31T18:56:41.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/microsoft-outlook-app.jpg?resize=1200,800",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/31/microsoft-tests-fix-for-latest-hours-long-outlook-outage/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-31-kalshi-bans-george-santos-for-life-over-state-of-the-union-b",
      "title": "Kalshi bans George Santos for life over State of the Union bets",
      "summary": "The disciplinary action comes two months after the Commodity Futures Trading Commission settled charges against Santos.",
      "body": "11:53 AM PDT · August 31, 2026\nImage Credits:Anna Moneymaker/Getty Images / Getty Images\nKalshi has banned former Republican Rep. George Santos from its platform for life, the first time the prediction market company has taken such an action.\nKalshi also penalized and suspended several political candidates accused of betting on their own races, including Ben Midgley, who ran and lost in the Republican primary for Maine’s gubernatorial race; Laurie Buckhout, a Republican congressional nominee; and former California gubernatorial candidate Stephen Cloobeck.\nThe Santos ban follows allegations that he bet on whether he would attend President Trump’s State of the Union address, allegedly making $17,839 on the wager. Kalshi’s compliance department said it “established reasonable cause” to believe that Santos had placed bets connected to his own attendance at the event. Kalshi also fined Santos $71,356 over the activity.\n“As a person capable of influencing the outcome of the underlying event, Santos was prohibited from trading in this market,” Kalshi wrote, accusing him of making public statements about his attendance to impact the odds.\nSantos responded on X: “Hey @Kalshi thanks for the lifetime ban from your gambling platform. Let’s see how much longer you guys are around for.”\nThe move follows a separate CFTC settlement two months earlier over the same conduct, in which Santos agreed to pay an additional $35,000.\nThe WSJ was the first to report on the ban.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Kirsten Korosec",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-31T18:53:41.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/GettyImages-1824575665.jpg?resize=1200,800",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/31/kalshi-bans-george-santos-for-life-over-state-of-the-union-bets/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-31-kushner-states-regret-over-world-cup-sell-off-plan",
      "title": "Kushner states regret over World Cup sell-off plan",
      "summary": "Venture capitalist Joshua Kushner says he would not have got involved in Fifa president Gianni Infantino's scrapped plan to sell off stakes in the World Cup if he had known how the football world reacted.",
      "body": "Venture capitalist Joshua Kushner, who was set to be the lead investor in Fifa president Gianni Infantino's scrapped plan to sell off stakes in the World Cup, says he now regrets joining the proposal.In his first public comments since the now-shelved Fifa Forward Enterprise (FFE) scheme sparked a major crisis for the governing body, Kushner said: \"Had we known what this would devolve into, we would not have gotten involved\".The brother of US President Donald Trump's son-in-law, Kushner added he had \"failed to appreciate the political dynamics of global football\".However, he also defended the proposal, saying, \"we stand behind the motivations of FFE\", and that its intention \"was to direct more capital and equity equally\" among Fifa's 211 member associations.Thrive Eternal, a firm founded by Kushner, had held talks with Infantino about heading up a $4.2bn (£3.1bn) investment in 20% of a new Fifa commercial subsidiary, before the idea was abandoned days after it was revealed in July amid a huge backlash.Kushner's comments come just days after Uefa asked a court in New York to approve subpoenas for testimony and documents from both the American billionaire and Thrive as it considers lodging a criminal complaint against Infantino in Switzerland. European football's governing body Uefa has led the rebellion against the Fifa president, who is facing calls to stand down from three confederations.In a statement, Kushner said: \"Money in football has historically been concentrated amongst a small group of countries.\"The idea behind FFE was to direct more capital and equity equally amongst all 211 member countries, providing significantly more investment to underdeveloped nations to nurture local talent, support grassroots football, enhance the fan experience, and ultimately grow the global game everywhere.\"It was an idea that every member association would vote on, not an obligation or determination.\"While we stand behind the motivations of FFE, we failed to appreciate the political dynamics of global football, and the lengths some would go to. Thrive has a long track record of being a partner to all constituents. Had we known what this would devolve into, we would not have gotten involved.\"In late July, shortly before scrapping the plan, Fifa said, \"Thrive Eternal...is expected to lead the proposed investor group for FFE\", claiming it would have seen the funding distributed to each national association for the 2027-2030 cycle increase from £5.9m to £14.7m.As well as Kushner, Uefa has asked for the disclosure of documents from two Fifa businesses in the US and American financier Greg Maffei, who was a key adviser on the deal.While Uefa says neither Kushner nor Maffei are anticipated defendants in any legal proceedings, it claims the pair first discussed the FFE concept in July 2025 and that Infantino had \"been discussing the underlying concept with Kushner for nearly a year before the terms sheet was ultimately signed.\"Its lawyers also argue the World Cup was deliberately undervalued at around £15bn, which it says is \"a figure that was neither the product of an open, competitive auction, nor tested by any independent valuer\". It claims Infantino failed to consult Fifa's hierarchy over the plan.Kushner has recently agreed a deal to buy basketball's Los Angeles Lakers for a record $12.5bn (£9.3bn).",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-31T18:38:55.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/ac98/live/245f6750-a568-11f1-a291-b542ee92de7c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/c780w17lw02o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-31-harvard-law-dropout-raises-6m-for-blue-voice-to-build-a-harv",
      "title": "Harvard Law dropout raises $6M for Blue Voice to build a ‘Harvey for police officers’",
      "summary": "Blue Voice is trained on department-specific laws, local ordinances, protocols, and guidelines that general-purpose AI tools can't access on the public internet.",
      "body": "David Lawrence was a Harvard Law student when an on-campus shooting involving a police officer sparked intense controversy over police conduct. Realizing that most policing errors occur when officers lack instant access to department rules, Lawrence dropped out to tackle the problem with AI. He teamed up with co-founders Amit Patankar, a Harvard MBA and former Google engineer, and Michael Gropman, a retired Boston police deputy chief, to launch Blue Voice — a Boston-based AI startup that provides real-time policy guidance to officers in the field.\nThree years later, Blue Voice is coming out of stealth with $6 million in funding led by SignalFire and Las Olas VC. Officers at 225 county agencies across 25 states now rely on the tool daily to ensure their actions follow department protocol — a platform Lawrence likens to Harvey, the AI tool widely used by lawyers, or OpenEvidence, which is a similar tool for doctors.\n“When an officer makes a decision, they do so from memory or best guess, based on thousands of pages of law, policy, municipal ordinances, and state laws that they’ve internalized and keep in their head,” Lawrence told TechCrunch.\nBefore Blue Voice, an officer who couldn’t recall a specific protocol — like the exact seventh step required at a crime scene — had few good options: Flip through a 15,000-page manual, wake up a supervisor in the middle of the night, or search generic tools like Google or ChatGPT that lack police-specific training. The problem was that traditional methods took too much time, while consumer AI models delivered incorrect answers up to 30% of the time, Lawrence said.\nBlue Voice says it solves this issue because it is trained on department-specific laws, local ordinances, protocols, and guidelines that general-purpose AI tools can’t access on the public internet. The platform also gives officers instant access to detailed school maps on their phones during active-shooter emergencies.\nToday, Blue Voice answers a question every minute. Lawrence says that officers feel confident relying on the tool because it always points them directly to original regulations rather than just generating an answer on its own the way ChatGPT would. Rather than dictating actions, the tool simply provides the laws, leaving the final decision to the officers, who combine it with their own field experience.\nOver the last year, says Lawrence, Blue Voice grew its customer base elevenfold because police departments see concrete results, including reduced crime and fewer operational controversies.\nBlue Voice recently helped prevent a kidnapping after a rookie officer spotted a man pressuring a young girl to enter his vehicle, according to Lawrence. Unsure whether he had legal grounds to intervene, the officer consulted Blue Voice on his phone. The app instantly confirmed that the situation met the legal criteria for “child enticement” — an offense covering attempts to lure a minor for the purpose of committing a crime against them — giving him the immediate authority to act.\nIn another instance, Blue Voice reminded a department chief that he couldn’t return an officer to active-duty following a shooting without first obtaining a third-party mental health evaluation.\nThe startup, which competes with PE-backed Lexipol, also recently developed functionality to help detectives solve cold cases.\nAt a time when police use of AI — such as Flock Safety’s license plate surveillance systems — draws growing criticism, Lawrence hopes Blue Voice proves that properly deployed AI can deliver public safety benefits without sacrificing civil rights.\nLawrence, who previously worked for Connecticut governor Ned Lamont, developed a deep passion for public service and says he has no regrets about leaving law school behind. “This is the best thing I could do with my career and the most impact I could potentially make,” he said.\nAbove, left to right: Michael Gropman, David Lawrence, and Amit Patankar.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nMarina Temkin is a venture capital and startups reporter at TechCrunch. Prior to joining TechCrunch, she wrote about VC for PitchBook and Venture Capital Journal. Earlier in her career, Marina was a financial analyst and earned a CFA charterholder designation.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Marina by emailing marina.temkin@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at +1 347-683-3909 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
