    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-08-30-brits-would-quite-like-their-private-messages-to-stay-privat",
      "title": "Brits would quite like their private messages to stay private",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.theregister.com/security/2026/08/30/turns-out-brits-would-quite-like-their-private-messages-to-stay-private/5292994\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49497063\nPoints: 276\n# Comments: 204",
      "body": "Polling finds two-thirds don't trust this government, or any future one, with access to their encrypted chats\nBrits have delivered a fairly unambiguous verdict on giving the government access to their encrypted messages: no, thanks.New polling commissioned by the Center for Democracy & Technology (CDT) found that 93 percent of British adults believe they have a right to private conversations online, while 89 percent think nobody should be able to access their personal messages without a court order.Perhaps more awkwardly for Westminster, two-thirds said they would not trust either the current government or any future one with the power to access encrypted messages.\nThat distrust crosses political lines. Among people who voted in the 2024 general election, 58 percent of Labour voters said they wouldn't trust any government with the power, alongside 59 percent of Conservatives, 56 percent of Liberal Democrats, 69 percent of Greens and 75 percent of Reform voters.\nPublic First did the asking, polling 2,000 British adults for CDT in April and weighting the results to reflect the wider population. The margin of error is 2.2 percentage points.The findings land as the UK government's appetite for slurping encrypted data continues to collide with the tech industry's insistence that encryption works best when nobody has a spare key lying around.That fight became particularly public when Apple withdrew Advanced Data Protection from UK users after receiving a secret Technical Capability Notice (TCN) under the Investigatory Powers Act. Apple challenged the order, and while the US government later said Britain had withdrawn its demand for access to Americans' encrypted data, reports have since suggested another TCN was issued focusing on British users.Despite the international row, 55 percent of those polled hadn't heard about the Apple notice at all. Once presented with the idea, enthusiasm remained thin.Just 12 percent backed the government being able to secretly order companies to provide access to users' information while preventing those companies from revealing the order. A third said the government shouldn't have that power at all, while another 41 percent wanted greater transparency or parliamentary oversight.Nor were respondents particularly sold on sacrificing security for law enforcement. 53 percent said the security risks of accessing encrypted messages outweighed the benefits, compared with 28 percent who thought the benefits came out on top.The reasons will sound familiar to anyone who has followed the encryption debate for more than five minutes. 84 percent worried that mechanisms allowing access to encrypted messages could introduce vulnerabilities for hackers and criminals, while 82 percent were concerned the powers could be abused.Knowing someone might be watching could also change how people behave. 65 percent said they'd become more cautious about what they liked, shared or commented on, while 41 percent said they'd self-censor criticism of public institutions or government officials.\nCDT is not a disinterested observer: the digital rights group campaigns for strong encryption and commissioned the research as part of that work. The polling itself, however, was carried out independently.Commenting on the research, Jim Killock, executive director of Open Rights Group, said: “The British public instinctively know that being able to communicate privately is crucial to our individuality and to the survival of a free and open society.“The government persists with the myth that it can weaken encryption to target the bad guys only. Attacks on the security of our phones, security tools and messaging apps harm us all and make our democracy weaker.”None of this is likely to end Westminster's long-running pursuit of encrypted communications. But if ministers were hoping the public was enthusiastically behind them, the numbers suggest otherwise. ®",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "defrost",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-30T09:26:08.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://image.theregister.com/255047.jpg?imageId=255047&x=0&y=0&cropw=100&croph=100&panox=0&panoy=0&panow=100&panoh=100&width=1200&height=683",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theregister.com/security/2026/08/30/turns-out-brits-would-quite-like-their-private-messages-to-stay-private/5292994",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-30-pc-harper-s-killers-to-stay-in-jail-as-pm-announces-changes-",
      "title": "PC Harper's killers to stay in jail as PM announces changes to early release scheme",
      "summary": "Andy Burnham tells BBC Breakfast anyone convicted of manslaughter will be excluded from the early release scheme in the Sentencing Act.",
      "body": "Prime Minister Andy Burnham has told the BBC that PC Andrew Harper's killers will remain behind bars as he announced changes to the early release scheme aimed at easing prison overcrowding.In an exclusive interview with BBC Breakfast, Burnham said anyone convicted of unlawful killing, including all forms of manslaughter, would be excluded from the scheme, which would have made two of the officer's killers eligible for early release.Burnham said extra prison space would be created by deporting foreign prisoners and releasing some inmates serving indefinite sentences.It follows months of campaigning from PC Harper's family and former colleagues to block the release of his killers.The 28-year-old was responding to a burglary in Berkshire in 2019 when he was dragged along a road by a vehicle as three teenagers fled the scene of a quad bike theft.When it emerged that two of the killers, Jessie Cole and Albert Bowers, could be released early, there were passionate calls for the government to intervene and block those convicted of manslaughter from early release.\"I heard obviously what Andrew Harper's family were saying, I mean who couldn't? Nobody would want a position to arise where those guilty of killing a police officer are let out early from prison,\" Burnham told the BBC. He conceded the announcement would mean \"hundreds\" more people stay in prison, meaning additional space will need to be found in a system that is more than 97% full in England and Wales.Harper's force, Thames Valley Police, said the announcement would be \"welcomed by many across policing and our communities\".Chief Constable Jason Hogg said in a statement, external he hoped it would bring reassurance to Harper's family, as well as to other victims and bereaved families \"who continue to seek confidence that the most serious offences are treated with the gravity they deserve\".\"Today is not about politics,\" he said. \"It is about remembering Andrew, recognising the profound impact his death continues to have on those who knew him, the communities he protected and those who serve in policing today.\"The prime minister said finding extra space would be achieved in part through ending Imprisonment for Public Protection sentences, or IPPs. These were available between 2005 and 2012, and were given to offenders who pose a significant risk of causing serious harm to the public. Once a minimum term has been served, the prisoner can be released only once the Parole Board is satisfied they no longer need to be confined for public safety, meaning many are being held indefinitely. Burnham said he had applied \"common sense\" on the treatment of IPPs, which he had \"long-standing concerns\" about.\"I was faced with a situation where people on those sentences who have more than served their time, not being released when others who've committed very serious crimes being released early,\" he told the BBC.\"The judgement I've made is the public, in my view, would prefer a situation where people who have served their time to be considered for release before those who haven't.\"According to Ministry of Justice data, 2,271 people are serving IPP sentences as of June. Of those, 856 have never been released because the Parole Board thought it was not safe to do so.Burnham conceded that of those offenders, there will be \"individuals who will pose a risk to communities\", and said a cross-party oversight group would be established.The government will table legislation to terminate the sentences by the \"end of this Parliament\", he added.Pressed on how many foreign prisoners could be returned, Burnham said it was \"hundreds\". Government figures show there were 10,134 foreign nationals in custody as of late June, representing 12% of the prison population.The government has said powers will be implemented early next year to deport some foreign criminals immediately after sentencing. Those powers would block foreign prisoners from ever returning to the country while \"keeping those in prison for the most serious crimes locked up for a substantial period before they are removed\".The government is not thought to have signed any additional prisoner returns agreements with other countries.It is understood that work has also begun to create more cells in existing prisons by adapting space used for other purposes, but that is also expected to take months.None of the measures announced by Burnham are likely to create much additional prison capacity by the time the first batch of prisoners are released early in October.The prime minister is said to have taken a \"political decision\" to use some of the remaining capacity in the prison system to ensure the changes announced today could be implemented immediately.In addition to those in jail for manslaughter, the prime minister said prisoners convicted of indecent assault offences and death by dangerous driving will also remain in prison.Historic child sex offences and causing or allowing the death of a child will also be exempted from the early release scheme, while new \"restriction zones\" to better protect domestic violence survivors from perpetrators released early will be introduced. The justice secretary is expected to give a more precise figure for how many people will be affected when he gives a statement in Parliament on Tuesday.In response to Sunday's announcement, Conservative shadow justice secretary Nick Timothy accused Labour of avoiding parliamentary scrutiny of the changes. \"The government must delay the scheme, bring forward emergency primary legislation and exclude the worst offenders properly,\" he said.Liberal Democrat justice spokesperson Jess Brown-Fuller MP said the uncertainty over the early release scheme had been \"unforgivable\". \"PC Andrew Harper's killers will now remain in prison, but this should never have been in question,\" she said. Dame Nicole Jacobs, Domestic Abuse Commissioner for England and Wales said she remains \"deeply concerned about the number of abusers who will still be eligible for early release\".\"Victims and survivors will still be forced to take measures to protect themselves from dangerous abusers because the government and our criminal justice system cannot\", she added.The Sentencing Act 2026 was designed to address the prisons overcrowding crisis, by releasing large numbers of inmates earlier than had been previously planned. The early release scheme is scheduled to start on 1 October.But it was met with fierce opposition from some, including family and former colleagues of PC Harper. Cole and Bowers were sentenced to 13 years for manslaughter in 2020 and would previously have become eligible for release under the scheme after serving half of their sentences, down from two-thirds.The driver of the getaway car, Henry Long, was given an \"extended\" sentence of 16 years for manslaughter and was therefore not eligible for release.While Downing Street initially warned it would not be possible to exclude \"one or two offenders\" convicted of manslaughter, Burnham later said he was \"confident\" of being able to block PC Harper's killers from the scheme.During his early days as prime minister, Burnham announced the changes brought in by the Sentencing Act would be paused while a review was carried out. In early August, further categories of offences, including rape, serious child sex offences and grooming, were excluded from the new policy, but that intensified pressure on the government to find additional space in prisons.Additional reporting by Jack Fenwick and Claire Keenan",
