    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-08-29-the-galaxy-z-flip-8-is-at-its-best-when-there-s-friction",
      "title": "The Galaxy Z Flip 8 is at its best when there’s friction",
      "summary": "The Z Flip 8 is designed to be used shut more than ever before. | Photo: Dominic Preston / The Verge\t\n\nWhat do you want from a flip phone when it's closed? A simple interface, perhaps notifications and a widget or two, for handling the basics? Or a fully fledged Android experience shrunk down for the Flip form factor? For years, Samsung has insisted on the former, even as some fans - and Razr riva",
      "body": "What do you want from a flip phone when it’s closed? A simple interface, perhaps notifications and a widget or two, for handling the basics? Or a fully fledged Android experience shrunk down for the Flip form factor? For years, Samsung has insisted on the former, even as some fans — and Razr rival Motorola —&nbsp;have pushed for the latter. With the Galaxy Z Flip 8, Samsung has given in.\nAlthough the Z Flip 8’s hardware is almost unchanged year-on-year, Samsung has fundamentally rethought how this phone is meant to be used when shut. The cover screen feels more like a regular Android phone, with easier access to the full versions of every single app, letting you browse social media, watch movies, and play games without ever opening the phone. Not every app works well on a tiny square screen with two cameras in the middle of it, but even when they don’t, that friction between me and the distractions of my phone still feels as much a feature as a bug.\n[Product: Samsung Galaxy Z Flip 8 | https://www.amazon.com/Samsung-Galaxy-Flip8-Unlocked-Graphite/dp/B0H12FP315/?tag=theverge02-20, https://go.skimresources.com/?id=1025X1701640&xs=1&url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.samsung.com%2Fus%2Fsmartphones%2Fgalaxy-z-flip8%2Fbuy%2F, https://bestbuy.7tiv.net/c/482924/614286/10014?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bestbuy.com%2Fproduct%2Fsamsung-galaxy-z-flip8-256gb-unlocked-graphite%2FJJGRF3TF7P]\nEverything exciting about the Z Flip 8 lies in its 4-inch cover screen, which is now more versatile than ever thanks to a homescreen redesign. Swiping down from the top left now brings up your notifications, while swiping from the top right instead shows a cover-screen-optimized version of the Quick Settings panel. Immediately, this feels more like a phone.\nMore exciting is what happens when you swipe up from the bottom: you get an app drawer. By default, this is populated with a selection of Samsung’s own apps that are optimized for the square screen, plus a “Labs” section with third-party options including Netflix and WhatsApp. But right below that, Samsung has included an easy prompt to install MultiStar, the Good Lock module that lets you run any app you’d like on the outer screen.&nbsp;\nSamsung used to bury the option deep in the phone’s settings, only to be found by those who knew to look for it. The bottom of the app drawer isn’t quite front and center, but it’s much more prominent than it used to be.\nYou can still run apps in three forms: full-screen, with the cameras blocking part of the software; a square display that stops just above the level of the cameras; or a thin portrait-aspect ratio that letterboxes the sides. A small virtual button at the bottom of the screen lets you cycle through them.&nbsp;\nGood performance isn’t guaranteed. Some apps, like Withings’ health tracker companion app, are almost impossible to use on the cover screen, with no configuration that leaves enough space for its graphs to fit. Google Maps is only a bit better —&nbsp;I love the idea of quickly checking my walking route without opening the phone, but there are so many floating interface elements that it’s unwieldy at best. Others work smoothly, but feel pointless: I can stream HBO Max on the tiny outer display, but I’d never want to.\nBut for other apps, the new cover screen options feel genuinely freeing. I can open Todoist and tick off a task, dip into WhatsApp to send a message, or open MyFitnessPal to log the snack I just ate. They’re quick actions that work well enough on the small screen, and because other apps — *cough* Instagram *cough* — don’t, I can dive into them without the risk of getting dragged into doomscrolling. That little bit of friction is just enough to save me from wasting 20 minutes I’ll never get back.\n[Image: Like last year’s phone, the cover screen wraps around the cameras. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/samsung-galaxy-z-flip-8-review-07.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nAs for the hardware, little has changed. The Z Flip 8 is 0.6mm thinner and 8g lighter than its predecessor. This is the thickest of Samsung’s three current foldables, but it’s more compact than Motorola’s Razrs, its only real rivals.\nSamsung didn’t upgrade the Z Flip 8 with the thinner, tougher titanium display stack found in the two new Z Fold 8 phones, which also means this has just as visible a crease as ever. It rarely bothers me, and is hard to see head-on, but you may feel differently. The IP48 rating is a more objective issue, and means that while this phone is mostly safe from water, it’s still vulnerable to dust.\nOn the inside, you’ll find the Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 if you’re in the US, though my UK model runs Samsung’s Exynos 2600. Exynos chips get a bad rep, but performance has been smooth so far, and I have no real complaints.\nAs with the Z Fold 8 phones, Samsung switched to more energy-dense silicon-carbon batteries for the Z Flip 8. Unlike those phones, it didn’t actually increase the battery capacity, but instead used smaller cells with the same 4,300mAh capacity —&nbsp;the key to shrinking the phone itself. Still, battery life is solid, reliably lasting the full day with battery to spare. The charging —&nbsp;25W wired, 15W wireless — is relatively unimpressive, and the only way to get Qi2 charging is still by using a compatible magnetic case.\nStorage and RAM remain the same as on the Z Flip 7; the screens do too apart from small bumps to peak brightness, and the cameras are also identical to last year’s. The 50-megapixel main camera is impressive, and especially helpful since the form factor means you can use it for selfies too. The 12-megapixel ultrawide and 10-megapixel inner camera aren’t remarkable, but they get the job done. A slab phone at this price would have better cameras, and most likely a telephoto lens, but the lenses here are good enough.\nThe question is whether it’s all good enough for the price, given Samsung has bumped the Z Flip 8 up to $1,199.99, a $100 increase. Z Flip 7 owners certainly shouldn’t upgrade, and anyone else should consider buying that phone instead, since the difference between the two is slight.\nAfter all, the big upgrades are software, and unlikely to remain exclusive. Last year’s Z Flip 7 hasn’t yet been updated to One UI 9, but since its outer screen is almost identical, it seems likely to get the upgraded software, giving one less reason to opt for the new model. The Z Flip 6 and earlier, with their smaller cover screens, seem more likely to miss out. I’ve asked Samsung about its upgrade plans, but haven’t yet heard back.\nMost of the other software upgrades aren’t even Flip-specific. Customizable, AI-powered cards have been added to the daily Now Brief; My FanCam lets you crop video to focus on a specific subject; and Audio Eraser can now dim background noise and highlight dialogue in apps like YouTube and Netflix. You’ll find those features across all of Samsung’s new foldables, plus the new Galaxy S26 FE.\nModest as the Z Flip 8’s hardware upgrades are, it feels like a turning point for Samsung, which is mostly done trying to limit the ways people use the cover screen. Given the speculation that the short, wide Z Fold 8 could replace the Flip form factor as Samsung’s “small phone that unfolds to a big phone,” it makes sense to close the gap between them.\nThe Z Fold 8 is a more capable phone than the Z Flip 8 when closed, its squat screen more usable than the Flip’s tiny one, despite Samsung’s software upgrades. But I have a real soft spot for the Flip form factor, which is tiny and pocketable when closed and a normal, almost compromise-free flagship phone when open —&nbsp;and endlessly satisfying to flip shut.\nMore than that, I enjoy its frictions. I welcome Samsung’s move to make it easier to use full apps on the cover screen, but I also welcome that it will never be entirely successful. Some apps simply don’t work on a screen this size, and the Z Flip 8 is all the better for it.\nPhotography by Dominic Preston / The Verge\nAgree to Continue: Samsung Galaxy Z Flip 8\nEvery smart device now requires you to agree to a series of terms and conditions before you can use it — contracts that no one actually reads. It’s impossible for us to read and analyze every single one of these agreements. But we started counting exactly how many times you have to hit “agree” to use devices when we review them since these are agreements most people don’t read and definitely can’t negotiate.\nTo use the Galaxy Z Flip 8, you must agree to:\n* Google Terms of Service\n* Google Play Terms of Service\n* Install apps and updates: “You agree this device may also automatically download and install updates and apps from Google, your carrier, and your device’s manufacturer, possibly using cellular data.”\n* Samsung End User Licence Agreement\n* Samsung Terms and Conditions\nThere’s also a variety of optional agreements, including:\n* Provide anonymous location data for Google’s services\n* “Allow apps and services to scan for Wi-Fi networks and nearby devices at any time, even when Wi-Fi or Bluetooth is off.”\n* Send usage and diagnostic data to Google\n* Google Gemini Apps Privacy Notice if you opt in to using Gemini Assistant\n* Turn on Samsung offline finding\n* Samsung news and special offers\n* Bixby personal data intelligence\nOther features, like Google Wallet, may require additional agreements.\nFinal tally: seven mandatory agreements and more than nine optional agreements.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Foldable Phones",
        "Gadgets",
        "Mobile",
        "Phone Reviews",
        "Phones",
        "Reviews",
        "Samsung",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Dominic Preston",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-29T13:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/samsung-galaxy-z-flip-8-review-13.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/986033/samsung-galaxy-z-flip-8-review",
      "readTimeMinutes": 8,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-08-29-when-fruit-is-scarce-these-monkeys-hunt-animals",
      "title": "When Fruit Is Scarce, These Monkeys Hunt Animals",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/when-fruit-is-scarce-these-monkeys-hunt-animals-the-behavior-may-offer-clues-about-the-origins-of-humans-meat-eating-habits-180989412/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49489274\nPoints: 19\n# Comments: 1",
      "body": "The bearded capuchins of northeastern Brazil typically eat plant-based foods, but they turn to small vertebrates when those sources are limited. Our ancestors may have done the same, eventually allowing predatory behaviors to be more frequently passed on in our lineage\nResearchers observed bearded capuchins in Brazil eating birds (shown here), lizards, rodents, opossums, snakes and other small vertebrates.\nThe bearded capuchin monkeys of northeastern Brazil eat a mostly plant-based diet, chowing down on palm nuts and other fruits. But when plant foods are scarce, the primates consume a surprisingly diverse smorgasbord of small animals, including snakes, opossums, iguanas, bats, rodents and bird eggs and nestlings, researchers report in a study published on August 26 in the journal PLOS One.\nThe findings reveal new details about the diet and behavior of these highly adaptable, long-lived monkeys. But they could also shed light on the evolution of human diets, including our ancestors’ shift toward eating more meat.\nHunting small prey seems to help capuchins cope with seasonal fluctuations in plant food availability—and “this may have also been the case for hominins,” study co-author Susana Carvalho, a primatologist at Gorongosa National Park in Mozambique, tells Science News’ Jake Buehler.\nCapuchins were observed eating birds (top left), marsupials (top right), lizards (bottom left) and snakes (bottom right).\nS. Carvalho et al., PLOS One, 2026 under CC-BY 4.0\nWhen the researchers started studying wild capuchins in the privately owned area of Fazenda Boa Vista roughly two decades ago, they primarily wanted to investigate the monkeys’ tool use and other complex behaviors. But while following two groups of the primates, scientists noticed that they were actively hunting and consuming small backbone-bearing creatures, or vertebrates—an intriguing behavior for a species that usually eats fruit. So, the researchers began making detailed notes whenever they saw the monkeys going after animals.\nThey ended up with more than 5,000 hours of observations between May 2006 and December 2010, recording 446 instances of capuchins eating animals. The team also looked at factors that may have affected the animals’ diets, including the age and sex of the individual monkeys that were chowing down on vertebrates, rainfall, temperature, humidity and the relative abundance of fruits and insects at the time.\nThe overlaps revealed some clear patterns. When plant foods became scarcer due to seasonal fluctuations, the monkeys seemed to hunt more. Lizards and rodents made up most of their prey, but the monkeys hunted a wide variety of animals. Scientists spotted males and females of all ages feasting on small vertebrates, but the behavior was more common among adult and juvenile males.\nAnd when their hunts were successful, the monkeys seemed to go for the brains and guts first, before eating the rest of the body.\n“This may allow them to obtain energy-rich fats and other nutrients quickly, which could be especially advantageous when prey is likely to be taken by another group member,” says study co-author Noemi Spagnoletti, a conservation biologist at Sapienza University of Rome, in an interview shared by the journal.\nDid you know? Tool use\nAmong primatologists, bearded capuchins are famous for using stone tools to obtain food. They’ve been observed placing palm nuts on a hard surface and smashing them with rocks to reveal the tasty kernels inside. Wild females have also been seen using sticks or blades of grass to pick their noses and eat any boogers they extract.\nChimpanzees often get the limelight for eating vertebrates, but a range of nonhuman primates also engage in the behavior, Margaret Bryer, a biological anthropologist at the University of Wisconsin-Madison who was not involved in the study, tells Science News.\nBut while chimpanzees are some of our closest living relatives, bearded capuchins and humans are only distant cousins, and the creatures are not considered a direct model for our ancestors. However, the monkeys share enough similarities with humans that they might offer some clues about the evolution of early hominin behavior.\nHumans are the only primates around today that regularly hunt animals larger than themselves—but how and why our ancestors evolved this behavior remains largely a mystery.\nOne hypothesis posits that our ancestors started eating smaller prey in times of seasonal plant food scarcity, before eventually leveling up to bigger game.\n“As late Pliocene and early Pleistocene eastern African hominin environments became increasingly seasonally arid, the availability of plant-source foods that are key to many primates’ diet in forest habitats would have only been available intermittently, whereas animal resources would have remained accessible throughout the year,” the researchers write in the paper. “This may have selected for a higher frequency of predatory behaviors in our lineage.”\nThe new findings seem to support this possible explanation, suggesting that hunting small animals might have been a “stepping stone to the exploitation of larger prey” for humans, Carvalho says in a statement.\n“The fossil record now needs re-analyzing to look at evidence of small vertebrate predation” by early humans, she adds.\nGet the latest stories in your inbox every weekday.",
