      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-28-neocloud-lambda-secures-1b-in-debt-to-buy-more-chips",
      "title": "Neocloud Lambda secures $1B in debt to buy more chips",
      "summary": "Neocloud Lambda has raised $1B in private debt to buy Nvidia AI chips and lease them to Microsoft. It's the latest in a string of loans, underscoring the high cost of the AI boom.",
      "body": "1:24 PM PDT · August 28, 2026\nLambda, an AI cloud company that buys computing chips and rents them out to businesses, has raised $1 billion in private, short-dated debt to buy Nvidia’s AI chips that it will lease to Microsoft, Bloomberg reports.\nThe terms of the deal, which Bloomberg says was arranged by JP Morgan Chase, signal that Lambda is betting it will be able to quickly deploy the chips and start generating revenue from them, letting it repay the debt fairly quickly using that incoming cash.\nThis is the latest in a string of loans that Lambda is using to fund GPU infrastructure for specific customers. In May, it closed a $1 billion secured credit facility, and this week it announced the closing of a $926 million loan to fund Nvidia GB300 GPUs, one of Nvidia’s newest chip models, for a deployment it’s under contract to provide Nvidia.\nThe $1 billion private debt deal comes as Lambda is reportedly in talks for a $3 billion pre-IPO round. The company last November raised $1.5 billion in venture capital at a $5.43 billion post-money valuation, per PitchBook data.\nLambda isn’t the only one relying on debt to fund the AI boom — according to data Bloomberg compiled, banks and tech companies have raised over $400 billion in AI-related debt globally in 2026 so far.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Rebecca Bellan",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-28T20:24:11.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Chip-chips-dollar-signs-money.png?resize=1200,960",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/28/neocloud-lambda-secures-1b-in-debt-to-buy-more-chips/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-28-i-learned-from-murray-boulter-won-t-make-wedding-ring-mistak",
      "title": "'I learned from Murray' - Boulter won't make wedding ring mistake",
      "summary": "Andy Murray has long been a guiding figure for British tennis, but newly-married Katie Boulter will not be following his lead when it comes to keeping her wedding ring safe as she prepares for the US Open.",
      "body": "Andy Murray has long been a guiding figure for British tennis, but newly-married Katie Boulter will not be following his lead when it comes to keeping her wedding ring safe.Three-time Grand Slam champion Murray, who married wife Kim in 2015, famously briefly lost his wedding band after playing with it tied to his shoe laces.Murray had left his trainers - with the ring still attached - under a car to dry out after a practice session in California in 2021, and after discovering they were missing the next day, was eventually reunited with both after putting out an appeal for their return.Boulter, who married Australia's world number seven Alex de Minaur this summer, said she will not be making the same mistake.\"I learned from Andy. I'm not going to do that,\" the 30-year-old said. \"I don't wear it. Not during matches.\"Boulter and De Minaur tied the knot at a small, private ceremony in England before holding a bigger celebration in Tuscany, Italy, in July.Speaking to BBC Sport, Boulter said: \"One was in the UK for my nearest and dearest. It was like eight people - very, very small, very much for my grandpa. \"It was a huge contrast to the Italian wedding, which had all of our closest people, all of our friends, and was a little bit more of a party and a lot of fun.\"When it came to De Minaur's speech, however, Boulter admitted she was \"sceptical\".\"I hadn't seen him do any preparation for the speech at all. And he was like, 'I'm just going to wing it. It's fine. How hard can it be?',\" she said.\"He was amazing. He was so, good. I was crying the whole time. I think I permanently cried for like three hours straight during all the speeches.\"Since their wedding, the world number 60 has wasted no time in throwing herself back into tennis before the US Open, which gets under way on Sunday.\"I actually just chose to go straight back into the conditions, go into the deep end and see what we've got,\" said Boulter, who lost in the first round in Washington and Toronto before suffering a second-round exit in Cincinnati.\"I do think the conditions in the States are very different from the UK. I think you can't really replicate them.\"So I felt like I'd be a little underprepared coming to the US and just getting used to the conditions again would have been quite difficult.\"Boulter's US Open begins with a first-round encounter against American wildcard Carol Young Suh Lee, who is ranked 143rd in the world.A record four British players have made it through qualifying to reach the main draw at the US Open.Fran Jones, Harriet Dart and Harry Wendelken all won their third and final qualifying matches on Friday, while Toby Samuel advanced on Thursday after his opponent, Chile's Cristian Garin, retired during a lengthy rain delay.It is the first time that four British players have progressed from qualifying at an overseas Grand Slam since the Australian Open of 1988.Jacob Fearnley lost to Austria's Jurij Rodionov in a rain-disrupted match that stretched across Thursday and Friday, but the 25-year-old will play Spain's Martin Landaluce in the first round after securing a main draw spot as a lucky loser.The five Britons will join compatriots Arthur Fery, Cameron Norrie, Jan Choinski and Boulter in the main draws.Wendelken earned his first appearance in the main draw at an overseas Grand Slam as he fought back to beat Lithuania's Vilius Gaubas 6-7 (3-7) 7-6 (7-5) 7-6 (10-5).He will face Italian 22nd seed Luciano Darderi in the first round, while Samuel is up against the Czech Republic's Tomas Machac.Jones, meanwhile, claimed a straightforward 6-4 6-1 win over Taiwan's Joanna Garland to qualify for a second successive year and set up a first-round meeting with Poland's Magda LinetteMeanwhile, Heather Watson missed out on her first US Open singles main-draw appearance in five years as Dart fought back from a set and a break down to win 2-6 6-4 6-1.\"It's never easy playing against a friend and Heather is such an a great player and has so much experience,\" Dart told BBC Sport.\"It showed for an hour and a half, I was really behind and she was playing some really good tennis. In the end I was able to stick to it somehow and find a way and turn it in my favour.\"Dart, who has reached the second round in her last two main draw appearances at Flushing Meadows, will play American Peyton Stearns in the first round.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-28T19:55:25.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/2ee3/live/67a94d80-a317-11f1-a291-b542ee92de7c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/tennis/articles/c20v7y3vy2do?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-28-disorder-at-balloon-release-for-man-who-died-in-a66-car-cras",
      "title": "Disorder at balloon release for man who died in A66 car crash 'put lives at risk', MP says",
      "summary": "Police now say they are not currently linking a deadly crash on the A66 to a fatal house fire.",
      "body": "Serious disorder at a balloon release for one of the young men who died in the A66 car crash is \"unacceptable\", an MP has said.Durham Police said a large crowd gathered on a privately owned field near Haswell, County Durham, on Thursday evening where five vehicles were set on fire and balloons, flares, and fireworks were released.Redcar MP Anna Turley said it put lives at risk, while Assistant Chief Constable Jon McAdam said the force condemned \"anyone who would use that tragedy as an excuse for disorder\".In an update on Friday afternoon, Cleveland Police ACC Dave Sutherland said the force was not currently linking the A66 crash to a house fire which killed a woman and a seven-year-old girl.It had previously said it could not rule out a link to organised crime in relation to the house fire and the crash.Earlier this week 17 people were arrested in connection with the investigation into the crash which killed PC Matthew Blades, 37, and PC Tom Clough, 38, Seven more men have also been arrested, bringing the total number of arrests to 24.Turley said the police force had been \"at pains to say we shouldn't think that these are all connected, and we shouldn't be jumping to conclusions\".The BBC understands the King has written to the families of two officers killed in the crash to express his deepest sympathies for their loss. Meanwhile Middlesbrough FC said it would ask supporters to pay their respects to the officers before Saturday's fixture against West Bromwich Albion.\"We are also showing our appreciation for the dedication and hard work of both men, and to Cleveland police force in protecting and supporting our local community,\" the club said in a statement.Seven people, including PC Matthew Blades and PC Tom Clough, died in the crash near Middlesbrough on Saturday. Two people were arrested on suspicion of murder following the house fire in Grangetown, near Middlesbrough, on Wednesday.With regards to Thursday night's disorder, Labour Home Office minister Turley said it \"put lives at risk\" and left residents \"frightened and angry in their own communities\".She added: \"People have a right to grieve and to pay their respects - but people also have a right to feel safe in their own homes and neighbourhoods.\"Durham Police said officers decided to let the cars burn because \"the field was contained and it was assessed there was no immediate risk to wider public safety\".County Durham and Darlington Fire and Rescue Service said one crew arrived at the scene but withdrew \"given the circumstances and concerns for the safety of our firefighters\".Police said a majority of the crowd in the privately-owned field near Haswell had travelled from outside the County Durham area, bringing with them a number of vehicles.The force said officers were called to the area shortly after 17:30 BST, adding that no-one was injured and no arrests were made.A spokesperson for the fire service said: \"Following a dynamic risk assessment, it was established that there was no immediate threat to public safety and no significant risk of the fire spreading.\"Given the circumstances and concerns for the safety of our firefighters, the decision was taken to withdraw from the area.\"Durham Police added a police operation, including investigation work and visible uniformed patrols, would continue in the area over the coming days.Turley called on Redcar and Cleveland Borough Council and Cleveland Police to use \"every appropriate power to protect our communities\".\"Seeing illegal bikes and quad bikes rampaging, and fires and fireworks being set off only heightens people's sense of fear and lawlessness,\" she said.\"While I understand people are grieving and upset, we cannot allow illegal activity to be excused and unchecked.\"Cleveland Police Federation chair Lauren Somerville told BBC Radio Tees earlier it had been \"more intense than ever in amongst pain and grief\", as an extra 200 police officers are being sent to support the investigation.\"It's been unimaginable... those feelings are going to continue for days, weeks, months and years,\" she said.Somerville said the extra officers would be used \"wherever is needed\".\"It's a testament to Tom and Matthew's memory that the whole country is behind us,\" she said.\"It's a real sense of people starting to understand what the sacrifice of policing is.\"The police officers died on Saturday when a Volkswagen Passat, which was going the wrong way on the A66 at South Bank, hit their vehicle.The five young men in the Volkswagen also died and inquests into all seven deaths opened on Thursday.Those arrested have been held on a range of offences including possession of a firearm and being part of an organised crime group.On Wednesday, Natalie McDonald, 34, died along with her seven-year-old niece Valentina Foster in the fire in Grangetown.A 48-year-old woman is still in a \"critical but stable\" condition and a 27-year-old man is also being treated in hospital, Cleveland Police said.Two teenage girls, who suffered non life-threatening injuries, have been released from hospital.Natalie's mother, Lisa Kirton, described her as \"a beautiful soul, who would have done anything for anybody\".Valentina, who was known as Tina, was staying at the house for the summer holidays and had been \"dancing around the living room\" only the previous night.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-28T19:38:35.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/1994/live/393622b0-a2dc-11f1-a291-b542ee92de7c.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cdj43zkjweyo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
