    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-28-violence-breaks-out-in-ceuta-as-migrant-crisis-continues",
      "title": "Violence breaks out in Ceuta as migrant crisis continues",
      "summary": "A group of migrants is accused of throwing stones at a military vehicle, while some locals set fire to their camps on the beach.",
      "body": "A month after tens of thousands of migrants entered the city of Ceuta, a Spanish exclave in North Africa, there have been outbreaks of violence as tensions mount between locals and foreign arrivals.The Spanish government has been accused by opposition parties of struggling to manage the crisis effectively.Ceuta's residents have complained of feeling unsafe on the streets, as well as expressing concerns about the burden being placed on public services.At least 72,000 migrants crossed from Morocco into Ceuta on 30 and 31 July. Most swam around a border fence and at least 100 people died during the mass crossing, according to figures from Ceuta's mayor.The vast majority of the migrants returned to Morocco within hours of their arrival, but several thousand remain in Ceuta, many of them children, and most are sleeping rough on beaches or streets.Anti-migrant demonstrations earlier this month were largely peaceful, but in recent days they have become increasingly violent.On Thursday, several dozen locals prevented Red Cross personnel from distributing food and water to the migrants. Afterwards, some set fire to migrant camps on Trampolín beach, the main site where they have been sleeping, and there were also clashes with the police.Those incidents came hours after the arrest of 12 migrants who were accused of throwing stones at a military vehicle, breaking its windows and injuring the four men inside. That same morning there were reports of vigilante groups pursuing migrants through the streets.Journalists and film crews have also reported being threatened.\"This is a city which has been under pressure, […] which has the right to normality,\" the mayor of the exclave, the conservative Juan Jesús Vivas, told Antena 3 television.\"The dignity of Spain is a stake right now in Ceuta,\" he said.The socialist-led government of Pedro Sánchez has appealed for calm and accused Vivas's People's Party (PP) and the far-right Vox of stoking the tensions. José María Figaredo, a member of Congress for Vox, is being investigated for a possible hate crime after saying, soon after the migrants' arrival, that they should be \"hunted down, one by one\".Interior Minister Fernando Grande-Marlaska told a congressional committee: \"We have heard during these weeks rhetoric and comments that contribute to the stigmatisation of immigrants and which feed social division at a moment when what we need is responsibility and statesmanship.\"Javier Celaya, who represents Ceuta in Congress for the PP, accused the government of treating \"the invaders as victims when the real victims are us, the people of Ceuta\".On Friday, all the parties in the Ceuta local assembly signed a declaration condemning violence.Earlier this week, the government centralised management of the situation, overriding the local authority, after criticism that it had been slow to respond.Defence Minister Margarita Robles also apologised to those in Ceuta who \"may have felt, or who even still feel, abandoned or not sufficiently cared\", as the government unveiled plans to overhaul the country's immigration laws.Much of the recent ill-feeling in the city appears to be driven by government plans to move migrants into existing public buildings, such as sports centres, in order to be able to process their legal status in an orderly way and return those not eligible for asylum to Morocco. Many local people, and Vivas, oppose this initiative, due to concerns that it will move the migrants closer to residential areas.The government is also facing questions about who was behind the mass migrant crossing. Politicians on the right and the far left have accused Morocco of involvement, pointing to its alleged use of the border with Spain in the past to exert political pressure. Sánchez and his Socialist Party instead blame people traffickers, insisting that Rabat is a reliable partner which facilitated the immediate return of most of the arrivals.Nonetheless, the crisis has placed the sovereignty of Ceuta, and its fellow autonomous city Melilla, under the spotlight. Morocco has maintained a longstanding claim to the territories, which it has reiterated in recent weeks.Spain's Foreign Minister José Manuel Albares said he \"roundly rejected\" such comments by Morocco. \"I don't speak about, nor have I spoken about, nor are we going to speak about Spain's territorial integrity with Morocco or with anyone else,\" he told the congressional committee.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-28T16:30:59.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/0b39/live/e5d60180-a2f9-11f1-8d5b-155abe464768.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cy9zj11gwzxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-08-28-trump-s-epa-wants-to-let-data-centers-hide-their-air-polluti",
      "title": "Trump’s EPA wants to let data centers hide their air pollution",
      "summary": "Just as new data centers face growing backlash from neighboring communities, the US Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is about to make it harder for people to weigh in on any pollution those centers create.\nThe EPA plans to toss out a federal rule requiring public notice and an opportunity to comment when certain industrial sites apply for an air permit. Advocates warn that the move would allo",
      "body": "Just as new data centers face growing backlash from neighboring communities, the US Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is about to make it harder for people to weigh in on any pollution those centers create.\nThe EPA plans to toss out a federal rule requiring public notice and an opportunity to comment when certain industrial sites apply for an air permit. Advocates warn that the move would allow data center developers and other industries to break ground without giving nearby residents a say, or even a warning that construction is planned.&nbsp;\n“You can see why that would be so interesting to an administration that wants to rush through the construction of data centers,” says Keri Powell, senior attorney and air program leader at the Southern Environmental Law Center (SELC). “What EPA would be allowing would be all of those facilities to go through and just not have to deal with the public, issue the permits in secret behind closed doors. Nobody knows what’s happening until the bulldozers are out there.”\nThe EPA’s proposed changes apply to a permitting process that’s been in place since the 1970s called New Source Review that applies to a wide range of facilities, from landfills and paper mills to power plant expansions. More recently with the rise of generative AI, these permits have also become a flashpoint for opposition to new data centers, as well as to power plants built to provide electricity for all those servers.\nIn July, the EPA announced that it would eliminate the federal public participation requirement for a large set of polluters and place that burden on states. The plan would leave it up to state and local air agencies to decide whether or not to notify the public about certain new sources of pollution — leaving advocates concerned that many of these agencies would forgo the process altogether. Air pollution is often regulated at the federal level because it can waft across the country, affecting residents in one state even if the source is in another.\n“The state and local authorities closest to the issues and the public should be making the decisions on the permitting process as much as possible, not Washington,” EPA administrator Lee Zeldin said in a statement at the time. “We are cutting unnecessary and burdensome red tape.”\nThe change applies to so-called “minor” sources of pollution, which the EPA says in its announcement “have relatively low emissions and limited environmental impact.” Powell’s group contends that the “minor” label is a misnomer and that the permitting process routinely covers projects that can have significant consequences for their neighbors.&nbsp;\nColossus 1, xAI’s massive data center in Tennessee that the company billed as the “most powerful AI training system yet,” applied for a minor source permit in 2025. That led to a hearing and elicited thousands of comments to a public notice posted by the local health department, Inside Climate News reported last year. The SELC and NAACP threatened to sue xAI for installing unpermitted gas turbines at the site the same year.\nSELC was one of nearly 200 health and environmental groups that filed comments last Friday asking the EPA to withdraw its proposal on New Source Review. They argue that by mandating public comment, residents can provide agencies with valuable insight on the repercussions of building a new data center or other industrial facility.\n“[The EPA’s proposal] could deprive the agencies making decisions about these [pollution] sources the necessary lived experience data of those communities. Without that information, it is impossible for permitting agencies to evaluate the full, cumulative impact of these facilities,” Kentucky Resources Council Senior Attorney Byron Gary said in a press release.&nbsp;\nThe public comment period for the EPA’s proposal ended last week, and EPA spokesperson Carolyn Holran says in an email to The Verge that they’re still reviewing all the comments submitted — more than 4,900 that need to be addressed before the agency can finalize its rule.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "Energy",
        "Environment",
        "Science"
      ],
      "author": "Justine Calma",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-28T16:28:40.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/06/STKS528_DATA_CENTERS2_C.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/986176/data-center-pollution-epa-rule-change-air-permit",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-08-28-dlss-5-leaked-and-modders-are-putting-nvidia-8217-s-ai-effec",
      "title": "DLSS 5 leaked and modders are putting Nvidia&#8217;s AI effects on everything",
      "summary": "The unofficial version of DLSS 5 makes Jesse Faden’s features much more pronounced. | Image: gabdeg via YouTube\n\t\n\nModders are trying out an unofficial version of Nvidia's DLSS 5 on Skyrim, Cyberpunk 2077, GTA V, and a bunch of other games after code for the AI upscaling tech appeared in an early-access build of NBA 2K27. Members of the RenoDX modding channel on Discord reportedly found a way to e",
      "body": "Modders are trying out an unofficial version of Nvidia’s DLSS 5 on Skyrim, Cyberpunk 2077, GTA V, and a bunch of other games after code for the AI upscaling tech appeared in an early-access build of NBA 2K27. Members of the RenoDX modding channel on Discord reportedly found a way to extract the DLSS “Neural Rendering” file and apply it to Control before it expanded to other games, as reported earlier by Notebook Check and VideoCardz.\nIn one video, a person shows how Control protagonist Jesse Faden’s facial features become more and less defined as they tweak various DLSS 5 settings enabled by the RenoDX modders. With the “Neural Uplift” setting disabled within the mod, Jesse’s face has a smooth, uniform look. But as soon as the modder turns this feature on with one of the presets, her face becomes paler, with more visible laugh lines and what looks like more makeup smudged beneath her eyes.\nAnother modder appears to have gotten the DLSS 5 tech working in GTA: San Andreas, where it seems to apply a filter effect to CJ’s face and enhances textures across the city. It also makes shadows on CJ’s face and his surrounding environment more pronounced, while the city’s lighting changes from a warm to a cool tone as the modder adjusts one of the sliders. Several other videos are floating around the web as well, with the DLSS 5 mod bringing more definition to the cartoony look of a Palworld character and altering the intensity of shadows. There are countless other videos floating around on the web (with more likely to come).\nThe DLSS 5 mod changes the lighting in GTA: San Andreas and makes CJ’s face look more defined. | Image: Jessica Natalia Mods via YouTube\nThe modders trying out the mod say that it impacts game performance. One person trying out the mod with Final Fantasy VII Rebirth says it “halves” their frames per second (FPS) and that “swapping between presets eats a TON of vram.” You can see the frames drop from 90 to 29 FPS as another modder adjusts the sliders in GTA V. Modders had the unofficial version of DLSS 5 running on RTX 50-series chips, but now some have gotten it working on RTX 4090 and RTX 4080 chips, too, according to VideoCardz.\nMuch like the reveal of DLSS 5, reactions to the unofficial version of the tech are mixed. While some players think the filters improve the appearance of games, others call them AI slop and say they look too much like an “AI-enhanced” filter you’d apply to images.\nNvidia first announced DLSS 5 in March, which uses AI to bring “photoreal lighting and materials” to game graphics in real-time. Demos showing DLSS 5 across Resident Evil Requiem, Starfield, and Hogwarts Legacy sparked immediate backlash, with many players complaining about characters’ “yassified” or generic looks. Others raised concerns that it could alter a developer’s vision for their characters, to which Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang responded by saying the tool “doesn’t change the artistic control.” DLSS 5 is set to officially launch this fall.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gaming",
        "News",
        "Nvidia",
        "PC Gaming",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Emma Roth",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-28T16:22:16.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/Screenshot-2026-08-28-at-12.07.16-PM.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,4.1233622463598,100,91.75327550728",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/games/986197/nvidia-dlss-5-leak-ai",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-28-a-lot-of-pressure-sse-airtricity-to-increase-gas-prices-by-1",
      "title": "'A lot of pressure' - SSE Airtricity to increase gas prices by 19%",
      "summary": "The annual gas bill of a typical household in the greater Belfast and west gas network areas will increase by almost £172.",
      "body": "Northern Ireland's largest provider of natural gas, SSE Airtricity, is to increase its prices by almost 19% from 1 October for its 200,000 domestic and small business customers.It means the annual gas bill of a typical household in the greater Belfast and west gas network areas will increase by almost £172.The company said \"unprecedented levels of volatility in global energy markets and higher wholesale gas prices\" had led to the price rise.Wholesale gas prices in the UK have effectively doubled since the US and Israel began their attack on Iran.Colin Broomfield from the Utility Regulator said the increase would be \"difficult news for many households and small businesses, particularly at a time when wider cost pressures remain a concern\". Glenn Davis owns The Upper Crust restaurant in the centre of Larne, along with his wife.He said energy price rises were putting a lot of pressure on small businesses.\"We are a daytime restaurant running six days a week and our gas bill is already sitting at about £400.\"We are also paying about £500 a week electric, so between the two it is getting hard to contend with,\" he said.\"It is a tough environment, I'm in the trade 30 years and this is as tough as it's been.\"All these rises are putting a lot of pressure on business in general but for small businesses and high street businesses it is just putting more and more pressure on.\"Isobel Russell from Larne said the prices put \"a lot of pressure\" on her because she lives alone and her husband is in a care home.\"I have to put most of his money into the care home, which doesn't leave me with a lot.\"But I still have to pay what everybody else is paying for my energy costs.\"She also said drilling should be expanded in the North Sea and that there should be more support from Stormont for people struggling.\"They're not giving us much support at the minute are they? I think they could do a lot more,\" she said.A Stormont scheme which will give eligible households a £100 voucher towards home heating oil costs will open for applications next month.Meanwhile, a separate scheme will give all households around £50 off their electricity bills in October.Broomfield, from the Utility Regulator, said the main reason for the increase in gas prices was \"the sustained rise\" in the wholesale cost of gas due to the ongoing conflict in the Middle East.\"The Iranian conflict has continued to impact energy prices globally for the past six months. \"In recent weeks, we have seen the wholesale price of gas reaching 169 pence per therm. This is twice as high as pre-conflict prices.\"Consumer Council for Northern Ireland head of energy policy Raymond Gormley said 19.2% is \"a significant amount\".\"We know electricity prices went up earlier this year and oil prices are high, so it will be a tough time for consumers. \"We knew SSE didn't put their prices up during the summer, so we knew this was going to be a big number and it comes in around where we would have expected.\"Gormley said there are a number of reasons for the increase, with the conflict in the Middle East being a huge factor. \"At the end of the day, before the conflict in Iran, gas prices were about 80p a therm and it is now more than double that, so we knew it was going to be a big number. It didn't come as a surprise to us, but it will be a shock to consumers.\" \"That area is a major oil and gas producer, so when those ships are being curtailed in the Strait of Hormuz, they are not getting out to their destinations. That means everyone is feeling the pinch and everyone else is competing for oil and gas.\"Gormley said the utility regulator has done a comparison with the rest of the UK and the Republic of Ireland, and he says comparatively \"we are about £60 more expensive than the rest of the UK, but about we are a few hundred pound cheaper than the Republic of Ireland\".",
