    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-27-extra-200-police-drafted-in-to-aid-force-investigating-wrong",
      "title": "Extra 200 police drafted in to aid force investigating wrong-way crash and fire deaths",
      "summary": "Cleveland Police confirms it has support from six local forces and the National Crime Agency.",
      "body": "An extra 200 police officers are being sent to support an investigation into a car crash and a house fire which left nine people dead, a council leader has said.Seven people, including two police officers, died in the crash at South Bank near Middlesbrough on Saturday, followed by a house fire in Grangetown on Wednesday that claimed the lives of a woman aged 34 and a seven-year-old girl.Alec Brown, leader of Redcar and Cleveland Council, said armed officers, dog units and \"community bobbies\" were among the additional personnel supporting local detectives.Cleveland Police said it was receiving support from neighbouring forces, but exact numbers were \"changing all the time\".It confirmed staff from the National Crime Agency, and the Northumbria, Durham, Humberside and North, South and West Yorkshire forces were supporting local officers.Earlier Prime Minister Andy Burnham said \"all of the support will be provided to get to the heart of what has happened\" over the past days.Earlier Brown told Radio 4's Today programme that residents deserved to \"feel safe in their own homes\" and he understood people from the area might feel \"left behind\".\"I'm from an estate, from the same ilk,\" he said. \"You live around people that may be suffering poverty or other social issues, it can be that uniform sort of consciousness that 'we're left behind'.\"But I can assure the people of Grangetown and South Bank they're as important to me as people from any other estate in the borough.\"Local Labour MPs Anna Turley, Luke Myer, Andy McDonald, Jonathan Brash and Chris McDonald issued a joint statement in which they welcomed the help of other police forces during the past week, but called for more long-term support to protect residents who had expressed \"safety concerns\" for \"far too long\". Myer said current national funding formulas for the police and fire service did not take into account the \"seriousness\" of the crime people in Teesside faced. \"We essentially have a metropolitan style of crime and also risk of fire but with a sort of rural-style funding,\" he said.\"This is why we need to see a real overhaul of the funding formula.\"Liberal Democrat leader Sir Ed Davey told the Press Association the events in and around Middlesbrough over the last week were \"deeply worrying\".Who were the seven killed in wrong-way crash?Two police officers among seven killed in wrong-way car crashPCs killed in A66 crash were heroes, say familiesFundraiser for A66 crash police officers tops £1mOn Saturday, PC Matthew Blades and PC Tom Clough died when a Volkswagen Passat, which was going the wrong way on the A66 at South Bank, hit their vehicle.The five young men in the Volkswagen also died and inquests into all seven deaths opened on Thursday.Police said 17 people, aged between 18 and 61, had been arrested across Teesside as part of an investigation into the lead-up to the crash. They have been held on a range of offences including possession of a firearm and being part of an organised crime group.Cleveland Police are also investigating another crash in which a vehicle hit the wall of a house in South Bank in the early hours of Thursday morning. The property had been empty at the time and the force said it had \"not yet been confirmed whether this incident is linked to wider organised crime\".On Wednesday, Natalie McDonald, 34, died along with her seven-year-old niece Valentina Foster in the fire in Grangetown.Natalie's mother, Lisa Kirton, described her as \"a beautiful soul, who would have done anything for anybody\".Valentina, who was known as Tina, was staying at the house for the summer holidays and had been \"dancing around the living room\" only the previous night. Lisa said Natalie's 15-year-old daughter and a friend had managed to escape after the friend \"kicked out a window\" upstairs.Natalie's brother, Josh Kirton, said she had not been involved with \"anything to do with gangs\" or the crash on the A66.\"That's what makes this so senseless,\" he said.\"There's some terrible people in the world and now my sister and Valentina have gone.\"The fire also left a 48-year-old woman in a critical condition in hospital, while another three people suffered non-life threatening injuries.Two men, aged 23 and 31, were arrested on suspicion of murder and remained in custody.Cleveland Police said it was \"keeping an open mind\" about the fire and said it could not \"rule out any links to wider organised crime at this time\".Follow BBC Tees on X,, external Facebook, external, Nextdoor and Instagram, external and listen to Radio Tees on BBC Sounds.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-27T20:14:57.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/3441/live/f48b6120-a204-11f1-b109-879e35c24276.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckgvnjpv1l0o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
