      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-27-openai-anthropic-google-and-100-other-companies-call-for-act",
      "title": "OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and 100 other companies call for action to defend against rogue AI",
      "summary": "Some of the world's largest tech companies and AI startups have come together to decry the current state of cybersecurity and to advertise a new solution that they say can ward off a new generation of cyber threats.",
      "body": "Over a hundred tech companies — including OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, and Microsoft — have signed an open letter urging both the private and public sectors to work together to defend themselves from AI-related cyber threats.\nThe letter — which was also signed by prominent cyber firms like CrowdStrike, Okta, and Fortinet, as well as prominent financial institutions and internet infrastructure firms — calls for the adoption of new forms of cyber defense, while also encouraging governments at the “local, national, and international levels” to collaborate on security.\n“In the coming months, AI-enabled cyber attacks will become far more widespread and sophisticated as models around the world become increasingly capable,” the letter states. “The companies and public services our communities depend on — from hospitals to water treatment plants to the infrastructure that powers the internet — are at risk.”\nThe problem AI poses to traditional cybersecurity defenses has been thrust into the spotlight lately by a string of bizarre incidents in which AI agents have attacked companies.\nThe Hugging Face incident — in which one of OpenAI’s agents autonomously broke out of its sandboxed environment and attacked the tech company — has been followed by a trail of other reported break-ins involving agents developed by other AI companies, including Anthropic and Meta. These incidents have bolstered the argument that the field of cybersecurity has been fundamentally altered and that bold new commercial solutions are necessary to mitigate them.\nThe letter further suggests the mobilization of a “collective response,” one in which “new partnerships” are formed “to raise security standards and find new solutions to emerging cyber threats.”\nSeveral of the AI companies that have signed the letter are still actively developing ever more advanced AI models, highlighting their conflicted position. At the same time, they are also offering programs to use frontier AI models for defensive purposes, including OpenAI’s Daybreak program, Anthropic’s Mythos, and Microsoft’s new cyber platform Perception.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nLucas is a senior writer at TechCrunch, where he covers artificial intelligence, consumer tech, and startups. He previously covered AI and cybersecurity at Gizmodo.\nYou can contact Lucas by emailing lucas.ropek@techcrunch.com.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Lucas Ropek",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-27T17:43:24.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/claude-app-centered-in-ai-apps-GettyImages-2167861906.jpg?resize=1200,801",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/27/openai-anthropic-google-and-100-other-companies-call-for-action-to-defend-against-rogue-ai/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-27-norwegians-lay-flowers-at-palace-as-king-harald-s-condition-",
      "title": "Norwegians lay flowers at palace as King Harald's condition worsens",
      "summary": "The 89-year-old's condition is described as \"extremely serious\" and members of his family have visited him in hospital.",
      "body": "Having been on the throne since 1991, King Harald is Europe's oldest head of state.Crown Prince Haakon, 53, the successor to the Norwegian throne, has been serving as regent while his father is on leave.King Harald has been treated in recent weeks with cortisone for haemolytic anaemia, a condition that causes the number of red blood cells in the body to be reduced, and leads to fatigue and shortness of breath.There was a sombre mood in Oslo as Norwegians followed the comings and goings of the royal family. Crowds milled around in Palace Square throughout the day as Norwegian public TV scrapped its schedule to cover the story.Although no-one in the royal family spoke, the message was clear that it had become an important moment to visit the king.Among those visiting the hospital were Crown Princess Mette-Marit who arrived with Haakon, as well as her son Marius Borg Høiby, who is under house arrest during his appeal against a conviction for rape.King Harald's sister, Princess Astrid, also visited the hospital, along with Haakon and Mette-Marit's children Princess Ingrid Alexandra and Prince Sverre Magnus.Prime Minister Jonas Gahr Støre cancelled a planned trip to Germany, where he was expected to meet Chancellor Friedrich Merz on Thursday.\"The whole nation is with us as we send warm thoughts to the king and the rest of the royal family,\" he said in a statement.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
