      "id": "bbc-2026-08-27-new-thunderstorm-warnings-for-parts-of-uk-as-rain-set-to-con",
      "title": "New thunderstorm warnings for parts of UK as rain set to continue",
      "summary": "The Met Office has issued fresh thunderstorm warnings for Thursday with the potential for England and Wales to see flash flooding as the unsettled weather continues.",
      "body": "Image source, Richard Sellers / PA MediaByBen RichLead Weather PresenterMet Office yellow warnings will remain in force for most of Thursday as downpours and thunderstorms move northwards across the UK.Southern England was first to see the rain overnight, with a yellow warning continuing until 08:00 BST in the south-east.A wider warning area covers all of Wales and large swathes of England and lasts until 22:00, with downpours bringing the risk of localised flash flooding and travel disruption.The wettest areas could potentially see 75mm (3in) of rain, accompanied by large hail, lightning and gusty winds.It will also feel rather humid for many.Spells of rain are set to continue over the weekend - a bank holiday for England, Wales and Northern Ireland - accompanied by windy conditions in the south.Image caption, The Met Office issued new warnings for thunderstorms on Wednesday evening. Flooding and travel disruption are possible in the yellow warning areasDownpours drifting northwards on ThursdayThursday morning is getting off to a wet start in southern England and south Wales.According to the Met Office, Herstmonceux in East Sussex recorded 21.4mm (0.8in) of rain in six hours overnight.The downpours, accompanied by lightning, hail and squally winds, will move north across Wales, the Midlands and parts of eastern England, arriving in northern England by the early afternoon.As this initial line of downpours clears northwards the skies will brighten and sunny spells will develop, but further scattered showers that could again be very heavy and thundery are possible.Some rain is likely to arrive in Northern Ireland and Scotland later in the day, although these northern areas of the UK are likely to avoid the very wettest weather.Within the Met Office yellow warning area, 20-30mm (0.8-1.2in) of rain could fall in just a few hours, with up to 50mm (2in) over a 12-hour period.Hills exposed to the easterly wind could possibly see 75mm (3in).Hazardous driving conditions are expected with a chance that roads will be closed and communities cut off.Lightning strikes could lead to power cuts, and hailstones of 1-2cm (0.4-0.8in) are also possible.The wet weather will be welcome news for the large areas of England and Wales that remain in drought, however there is the increased risk of flash flooding where the ground is very dry and hard.Not enough rain will fall to alleviate the recent dry spell, with experts warning that months of persistent wet weather is needed to replenish water supplies.A weekend of events - and more rainThe coming weekend brings a number of festivals and high-profile events.Reading and Leeds Festivals both run from Thursday until Sunday, as does Creamfields in Cheshire. Manchester Pride takes place from Friday until Bank Holiday Monday, while Saturday marks the start of Notting Hill Carnival in London, which also continues until Monday.Image source, Joseph Okpako / Getty ImagesImage caption, Festivalgoers at Reading will be hoping to avoid a repeat of these soggy scenes from 2024The weather looks decidedly mixed for these events, with a fresher feel, brisk winds - especially in the south - and the likelihood of rain at times.A zone of wet weather will move eastwards across England and Wales on Saturday, although drier and brighter conditions may spread from the west later. Northern Ireland and Scotland will be a little drier with some sunny spells, but there is still the chance of a few showers.More heavy showers will move eastwards on Sunday, this time affecting most parts of the UK. Some of these could be heavy and thundery, with wind gusts touching 40-45mph (64-72km/h) in the far south-west of England later.Temperatures will tend to range from 16C (61F) in northern Scotland to 23C (73F) in southern England, which is about normal for late August.Low pressure remains in charge on Monday meaning strong winds and further showers, perhaps heaviest across the north of the UK.There is no sign of a return to the dry and hot weather that has dominated this summer.Whatever your plans for the weekend, you can keep up to date with the forecast by checking the BBC Weather website and app.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-27T05:11:35.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_weather/1200/cpsprodpb/a913/live/84272130-a1dc-11f1-a291-b542ee92de7c.jpg",
