      "title": "Business Daily",
      "summary": "Evergrande was at the heart of China’s property boom, and its dramatic collapse",
      "body": "Home            News            Sport            Weather            iPlayer            Sounds            Bitesize     CBeebies     CBBC     Food         Home     News     Sport     Business     Technology     Health     Culture     Arts     Travel     Earth     Audio     Video     Live          Use BBC.com or the new BBC App to listen to BBC podcasts, Radio 4 and the World Service outside the UK.Find out how to listen to other BBC stationsEpisode detailsWorld Service,·27 Aug 2026,·23 minsPower players: Evergrande and the unraveling of China’s property boomBusiness DailyAvailable for over a yearFor years, China’s property boom helped drive the country’s extraordinary economic growth. At the centre of it was Evergrande, which grew from a regional developer into a sprawling corporate empire, and one of the world’s most indebted companies.\nThen the debts caught up with it. Evergrande’s collapse exposed the vulnerabilities of China’s property model, helping push the wider housing market into crisis and weighing on the country’s economy.\nNow its founder, Hui Ka-yan, has been sentenced to life in prison for crimes including bribery and illegal use of funds.\nWill Bain and Rahul Tandon are joined by Jennifer Pak in Beijing to explore how Evergrande became so big, what went wrong, how its collapse exposed the problems in China’s property model, and what it means for the wider global economy.\n(Photo: China Evergrande Group then-Chairman, Hui Ka Yan, attends a news conference on the property developer's annual results in Hong Kong, China 28 March, 2017. He has short black hair with a side parting and wears a white shirt with a red patterned tie and a black suit jacket. In the background is a city skyline of skyscrapers, reflected in blue water. Credit: Bobby Yip/Reuters)\nYou can email the team: businessdaily@bbc.co.uk\nPresenter: Will Bain, Rahul Tandon and Jennifer Pak\nProducer: David CannProgramme WebsiteMore episodes",
