      "id": "hackernews-2026-08-26-the-tariff-cost-analysis-of-the-costs-to-americans-from-new-",
      "title": "The Tariff Cost: analysis of the costs to Americans from new tariffs on Canada",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://thetariffcost.com/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49453161\nPoints: 58\n# Comments: 33",
      "body": "Know someone who should see this?\nAnd Canada is taxing you back\nWhat Canada taxes are American exports, the things American workers grow and build.\nToday the list is steel and almost nothing else. September's includes groceries.\nTariff lines by product category\nEvery line Canada taxes today is 25%. September adds a 50% tier and puts\nhundreds of American steel and aluminum lines in it.\nTariff lines by rate, from September 8\nEvery American product on Canada's list, all — of them.\nFilter it, or search for something specific.\nThese numbers are public. Almost nobody has seen them. If you know\nsomeone who should, send them the link.\nEvery number on this page is measured, with one exception.\nNothing here is a forecast or a projection. The exception is What tariffs cost\nyour household, above, which needs an assumption no dataset can supply. It says\nso at the top of the section and carries a label wherever it appears. Elsewhere, when\na question could only be answered by assuming something, we say so and leave the\nSix public datasets, all free, all citable:\nDuty actually collected on Canadian goods, and the value it was\ncharged on — U.S. Census Bureau, timeseries/intltrade/imports\nU.S. exports to Canada by state and commodity —\nU.S. exports to Canada by state and industry —\nU.S. energy imports from Canada by state —\nU.S. Census Bureau, imports/statehs, HS 2709/2710/2711/2716.\nHousehold count — U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 1-year 2024, table B11001.\nCanada's counter-tariff list — Department of Finance Canada,\ncomplete list of U.S. products subject to counter tariffs.\nTwo tariffs, two payers. Canada's counter-tariffs are paid by Canadian\nimporters and show up in Canadian prices. What they hit on this side is American\nexporters, the people who make what is on the list. Washington's tariffs on Canadian\ngoods are paid at the American border by American importers, and that is the duty\ncounted in What you have already paid. Two different taxes with two different\npayers. Do not add them together.\nWhat this page does not tell you, because the data cannot:\nHow much of the duty on Canadian goods reaches a shelf price. That is\npass-through, and it is a margin decision made firm by firm. The Canada figures above\nassume nothing about it. They are duty collected at the border. The one modelled\nnumber on this page does state a pass-through rate, but for tariffs on all U.S.\nimports rather than Canada's alone. See below.\nWhat a tariff will do to any future price, at the pump or anywhere else.\nHow many jobs are at risk. Turning export dollars into a job count needs an\nattribution model we do not have.\nWhat share of any industry's exports is tariffed. The tariff list is classified by\nHS and the industry data by NAICS; joining them takes a concordance we do not have,\nand guessing one would manufacture a number.\nCounting tariff lines tells you how wide the list is, not how much money is on it. One\nline can carry a billion dollars of trade or almost none. That is why the dollar figures\ncome from trade data instead of from the list.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "mikestorrent",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-26T17:54:51.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://thetariffcost.com/share/card.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://thetariffcost.com/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
