      "id": "bbc-2026-08-26-door-was-locked-at-pakistan-hospital-where-fire-killed-14-ba",
      "title": "Door was locked at Pakistan hospital where fire killed 14 babies, witnesses say",
      "summary": "The hospital tells the BBC the ward door was not locked but manned with security guards, to protect the babies.",
      "body": "Figure caption, Watch: Hivsa Walid describes grabbing newborn and fleeing from hospital as fire ragedByCarrie Davies and Usman ZahidReporting fromPIMS hospital, IslamabadIt was just after dawn on Wednesday morning at a maternity ward in the Pakistani capital when a horrific blaze erupted.It would go on to kill 14 infants as parents say they waited hours for help to arrive. Hivsa Walid was near the room at the Pakistan Institute of Medical Sciences (PIMS) hospital with her sister-in-law, who had given birth days earlier. She initially heard a shout and thought a patient had died - but then she saw smoke. The pair fled with the baby, who is so small she does not yet have a name.\"We didn't even think about our belongings. We just grabbed the baby and rushed downstairs.\"Some patients had delivered their babies just that night. I don't even know how we managed to get down - it was all a blur.\"The fire broke out in an air-conditioning unit on the third floor of the mother and child unit at PIMS, one of the main state-run hospitals in Islamabad. Only one of the 15 babies present was saved. Image source, ReutersImage caption, Eyewitnesses say proper evacuation protocols were not in place at the hospitalKhurram Mehmood, 40, told AFP news agency there was a long wait for rescue workers. \"I lost my daughter. She was only three days old,\" he said.Jaweria, the mother of another victim, told AFP news agency the door to the maternity ward was locked.\"For two hours I kept crying and wailing,\" she said. \"Police arrived after four hours and said they are here to investigate.\"\"Why did the doctors leave the children there?\" she asks. \"To hell with you, rescue team and police. Babies burnt to death there, but nobody could save them.\"Rana Imran Sikander, the hospital's executive director, told the BBC the ward's door was not locked but was manned. He cited the need to protect babies from being stolen, something that has happened before at government hospitals.Figure caption, Scenes outside Pakistan hospital where newborn babies were killed in fireWalid suggested PIMS's safety protocols were inadequate, despite it being a major hospital. \"There should have been fire extinguishers and protocols, but no one was doing anything - everyone was just running.\"The father of a baby who died told Reuters he had been visiting the ward for eight days and the building had \"no fire security, no proper exits\". \"All doors were closed, only one door was for us to visit our children. They kept the other doors locked because they feared we would come to see our children too often.\"Several eyewitnesses have claimed duty staff were not around.People had to \"break the door down\" and rescue teams were forced to smash the windows to control the flames, a witness who did not give her name told AFP.Abdul Ghafoor, another witness, told AFP he tried to get into the building to rescue patients but couldn't reach the maternity ward.\"There was thick black smoke inside and patients were trapped,\" he said. \"Rescue teams arrived after almost two hours.\"Islamabad's Chief Commissioner Sohail Ashraf said rescue teams reached the scene \"immediately\" after the emergency call was first made, according to AFP.PIMS is one of the biggest hospitals in Pakistan's capital, with 156 beds in its maternal and child health unit.The country has dealt with a number of major fires in recent years, with poor safety laws, building codes and lax enforcement often blamed for steep death tolls.Survivors of a fire which killed dozens at a Karachi shopping mall in January this year said locked exits probably contributed to the high number of fatalities.Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif has expressed his \"deep sorrow and heartfelt regret\" over the latest blaze, and instructed authorities to \"conduct an immediate investigation\", according to a statement from his office.He has also suspended Federal Health Secretary Aslam Ghauri, a top official in the health department.\"Such a situation with children is irreparable,\" the statement said.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-26T14:42:03.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/3829/live/3df24620-a166-11f1-a291-b542ee92de7c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/czdz13elezlo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-26-reform-proposes-30-tax-rebate-for-hmrc-phone-delays",
      "title": "Reform proposes £30 tax rebate for HMRC phone delays",
      "summary": "People on hold to HMRC for more than 30 minutes would get a tax credit under plans announced by Robert Jenrick.",
      "body": "People on hold to HM Revenue and Customs (HMRC) for more than 30 minutes would get £30 off their tax bill under a Reform UK government, Robert Jenrick has announced.Reform's economy spokesman said HMRC's treatment of taxpayers was \"offensive to working people\" and his party would tie the pay of senior HMRC officials to customer service targets.To prevent abuse, the tax credit for phone delays would be limited to two occasions in a given tax year, Jenrick said.HMRC said its customer service had \"vastly\" improved over the past two years, with average call waiting times now 11 minutes and more of its services being delivered online or through its app.In January 2025, the Public Accounts committee accused HMRC of seeking to \"degrade its telephone service to drive taxpayers to digital channels\" in a highly critical report., externalThe MPs' report said nearly 44,000 customers were cut off without warning after being on hold for more than an hour in 2024 and \"HMRC's treatment of taxpayers has damaged trust in the tax system\".Jenrick highlighted the findings - which were rejected as \"completely baseless\" by the then boss of HMRC - at a media conference in central London.\"Frankly, it is offensive to working people and I have had enough,\" said the former Tory MP.\"So, we will give HMRC a big incentive to actually provide proper customer service.\"I'm announcing today that under a Reform government, if you spend more than half an hour waiting for HMRC to answer your call, you will get a £30 credit off your tax bill. If HMRC delay, they can repay.\"And to make sure that the staff actually hit their targets, we'll tie the pay of senior HMRC officials to their customer service performance.\"The HMRC said 80% of customer interactions now took place digitally, reducing the time spent on the phone to advisers, who were there to deal with vulnerable or digitally excluded customers or those with particularly complex problems.A spokesperson said: \"Our customer service performance has improved significantly with average call wait times now around 11 minutes. \"Overall customer satisfaction is at around 80% with more people than ever using our digital services, including millions using the HMRC app.\"Jenrick also pledged to scrap UK privacy rules in favour of a \"light-touch\" approach to data protection as part of a bid to slash \"red tape\" for businesses if Reform wins power.He said the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), which mirrors EU legislation and was adopted as domestic law after Brexit, had \"strangled\" tech firms and small enterprises.The framework would be replaced by rules modelled on New Zealand's privacy legislation, which Reform says has the lightest-touch regime still recognised as being \"adequate\" by the EU.Labour accused Reform of wanting to \"scrap vital safeguards that protect people's private data\" with its \"unworkable and unserious plans\".",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-26T14:41:02.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/3d5b/live/551fe6a0-a147-11f1-a291-b542ee92de7c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c804rd7leppo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-26-bill-gates-wants-to-see-a-robot-tax-and-human-reserved-jobs-",
      "title": "Bill Gates wants to see a robot tax and ‘Human Reserved’ jobs to mitigate harms from AI",
      "summary": "Gates is mostly in the Responsible AI camp, but there are a few ideas in here we hadn't heard before.",
      "body": "7:37 AM PDT · August 26, 2026\nBill Gates posted a long essay to his Gates Notes site today, showing just how much the Microsoft co-founder has been thinking about the social impacts of AI.\nGates is mostly in the Responsible AI camp, arguing that Pacing the Frontier, the open letter published by AI employees pushing for an AI slowdown, would be a good idea, but expressing skepticism that it would be sustainable.\nHe also expressed excitement about AI’s benefits to science and healthcare, but worried about the labor impacts — all pretty familiar stuff if you follow Anthropic’s policy work.\nBut there were a few genuinely new ideas that could change the conversation if they take off. For starters, he proposes a “robot tax”:\nRight now, if you’re an employer and you hire someone, you pay payroll taxes on their earnings. But if you buy a robot, you can usually write it off right away as a business expense. The tax system nudges you toward replacing people with machines. A tax would slow the rush away from human labor a little and raise money for retraining and a stronger safety net.\nHe also proposes setting aside certain jobs as “Human Reserved,” essentially barring AI from being used in certain tasks. It’s an interesting idea, and one that would be easy for policy-makers to enact:\nWe might set something aside as Human Reserved for economic reasons. For example, we may do it because allowing machines to take over a certain role will displace a large number of people who can’t easily change jobs. You can’t tell a 55-year-old who has worked in construction their whole career that they need to go work at an elder care facility and expect them to find it fulfilling. Sometimes the decision to make something Human Reserved will be driven by other factors. In health, for example, imagine a robot giving you the awful news that you have an incurable disease. There’s no technical reason why it couldn’t. Yet it shouldn’t.The Human Reserved domain will evolve over time — for example, we should consider setting aside some jobs now and phasing in AI slowly over years or decades with a commitment to preserve some jobs.\nWe should also note that both ideas would put a pretty serious dent in the profits of the major labs, which may be why we haven’t heard much about them until now.\nIn addition, there are still a lot of unanswered questions about who exactly would set these rules and what exactly the rules would say, if they were to be rolled out.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
