      "id": "theverge-2026-08-25-trump-is-upping-the-price-of-big-tech-s-favorite-visa",
      "title": "Trump is upping the price of Big Tech’s favorite visa",
      "summary": "In the span of a few hours on Monday, the Department of Homeland Security announced that it would be implementing a fee of over $103,000 on H-1B visas, and news broke that the State Department plans on revoking the visas of up to 200,000 asylum seekers - which, according to The Associated Press, would be the largest mass visa revocation in US history. \nMore than a year into President Donald Trump'",
      "body": "In the span of a few hours on Monday, the Department of Homeland Security announced that it would be implementing a fee of over $103,000 on H-1B visas, and news broke that the State Department plans on revoking the visas of up to 200,000 asylum seekers — which, according to The Associated Press, would be the largest mass visa revocation in US history.&nbsp;\nMore than a year into President Donald Trump’s second term, it’s become clear that the only way Trump can get the deportation numbers he wants is by stripping legal immigrants of status. Beyond having ICE arrest, detain, and deport immigrants, the administration is pressuring noncitizens to “self-deport,” in some cases imposing civil fines on them and threatening to seize their assets. It’s creating legal hurdles not only for new legal immigrants but for those already in the country, and in the process, likely hurting businesses and social services nationwide.\nH-1B visas are often seen as synonymous with Big Tech, in no small part because Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft are the three biggest employers of H-1B workers. A federal judge struck down Trump’s previous attempt to raise the cost of these high-skilled employment visas in June, but not until over 70 employers paid the $100,000 fee. Now Trump wants to raise the cost to $103,265. Advocates and business leaders are worried Trump will soon come after the Optional Practical Training (OPT) program, which DHS hinted may soon carry a $100,000 fee of its own. OPT lets international students work in their field of study for 12 months, with extensions of up to 24 months for some STEM students and additional extensions for holders of certain graduate degrees.\n“This is clearly unlawful,” said Todd Schulte, the president of FWD.us, an immigration reform organization founded by Mark Zuckerberg and other tech executives in 2013. “We don’t have to guess what this is for. This is to get rid of the OPT and H-1B program, which will have upstream and downstream devastating consequences: companies will be started elsewhere, people will be hired elsewhere.”\nThis won’t just hurt the tech industry. The previous fee hike exacerbated labor shortages at rural hospitals across the country and Alaska public schools, both of which have increasingly turned to high-skilled immigrant workers in the wake of local population decline. If the $100,000 fee was an inconvenience for Amazon and Meta, it was utterly catastrophic for rural communities already having trouble attracting qualified candidates.&nbsp;\nForeign workers aren’t the only legal immigrants Trump is targeting. According to the AP, which broke the news, the State Department will rescind up to 200,000 business and travel visas issued between 2016 and 2026 to people who applied for asylum after arriving in the US. Beyond limiting their ability to travel outside the country, this move won’t change much for asylum seekers who came to the US on nonimmigrant visas — at least not for now. But Schulte said the visa revocations are part of Trump’s efforts to gut the humanitarian immigration system. “Every single other pathway is harder, outlawed, eliminated, more bureaucratic, more difficult across the board,” he said. “It’s an all-of-government approach.”\nThe administration is framing this change as a way of closing a loophole in the asylum system: people coming to the country on nonimmigrant visas, ostensibly for work or pleasure, only to ask for asylum upon setting foot in the country.\n“We are coordinating with DHS to identify and revoke the nonimmigrant visas of foreigners who have come to the United States claiming to be short-term visitors, but then file for asylum to stay here permanently,” State Department spokesperson Tommy Pigott told the AP.\nBut the people who applied for asylum this way didn’t break any laws. In fact, a person needs to be present on US soil to apply for asylum; there’s no way to apply from outside the country. And Trump has closed most other avenues for humanitarian migration. There’s an effective “asylum ban” in place at the US-Mexico border. Refugee admissions have dropped to record lows, and the administration is now prioritizing the resettlement of white South Africans and denying almost everyone else.&nbsp;\nIn his second term, Trump has shown that the only immigrants he wants are those who look a certain way — or who can pay a small fortune for the privilege of working in America.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Analysis",
        "Policy",
        "Politics",
        "Report"
      ],
      "author": "Gaby Del Valle",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-25T21:45:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/09/257976_h1b_visa_chaos__CVirginia.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/policy/984723/trump-h1b-fee-asylum-legal-immigration",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-08-25-python-s-pre-declared-constants-are-kinda-weird",
      "title": "Python's pre-declared constants are kinda weird",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://sebsite.pw/w/20260801-pythonconstants.html\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49441033\nPoints: 123\n# Comments: 68",
      "body": "python has 6 pre-declared \"constants\": True, False, None, __debug__, Ellipsis (or equivalently ...), and NotImplemented. but they all behave slightly differently, for some reason.\nTrue, False, and None are keywords. they aren't identifiers, they're just straight up their own lexical tokens. which is really weird; nothing else is like this in python. usually stuff is resolved during regular name resolution, not in the lexer itself.\nan interesting side effect of this is that expressions like x.True raise a SyntaxError. i'm curious as to what the rationale was for this decision (if there was one).\nthere's some more interesting stuff with these constants, but i'll get to it later, since it ties in with the other constants.\n__debug__ is a boolean constant: it's normally True, but when running with -O, it's False. the idea is similar to how assert is disabled in non-debug builds: you can wrap code in if __debug__ if the check would be too expensive in an \"optimized\" build, or something.\n__debug__ is really interesting though, because although it's a normal identifier (unlike True, False, and None), it's the only identifier in the language which can't be assigned to:\nSyntaxError: cannot assign to __debug__\nyou can't even assign to it as an attribute:\nSyntaxError: cannot assign to __debug__\nagain, no other identifier behaves like this. this is a true special case.\nbut because it's not a keyword, it behaves slightly differently to True, False, and None:\nx.__debug__ raises AttributeError (rather than SyntaxError), since it's syntactically valid; it's just looking up an attribute which doesn't exist.\ninterestingly, there's also a special error message for attempting to delete __debug__ (despite the fact that this would raise a NameError anyway if not for the special case), but this doesn't apply for deleting an attribute named __debug__:\nTraceback (most recent call last):\nFile \"\", line 1, in\nNameError: name 'x' is not defined\nif x were defined, an AttributeError would be raised instead. in either case, it's not a SyntaxError (unlike assignment), for some reason.\ntangent: SyntaxError is a lie\nspeaking of errors: assigning to __debug__ is one of only a few cases i'm aware of where a SyntaxError is raised despite something not actually being invalid syntax. here, you can confirm it yourself:\n>>> assert (__debug__ := 67)\nrunning that assert in a debug build raises a SyntaxError, but with -O, the assertion is never compiled, and so no exception is raised.\ntwo other instances of this are using yield or await outside of a function:\nEllipsis and NotImplemented are documented in the \"constants\" section of the reference, but unlike the other 4 constants, they aren't \"real\" constants. they're just normal builtins, so they can be shadowed by globals:\nagain, i'm curious about the rationale here. why is it that these aren't special, but the other constants are?\nhere's something interesting: despite being lexical tokens, True, False, and None also exist as normal builtins:\n>>> getattr(builtins, 'None') is None\nthere's no way to directly access these without using getattr.\nbut here's where things get really interesting: setattr also works!\nhowever, this doesn't change the value when accessed with the lexical token:\nbut __debug__ has the same behavior!\nso __debug__ can sorta be assigned to, but despite not being a lexical token, it's special cased just like True, False, and None: its value is unaffected by changes to the builtins module. so it really is a constant!\nEllipsis and NotImplemented are, once again, not actually constants:\nthis doesn't change the value of ... though:\nso in some sense, ... is a real constant, but Ellipsis isn't. weird, right?",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "rbanffy",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-25T21:39:02.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://sebsite.pw/w/20260801-pythonconstants.html",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-25-trump-says-all-mines-cleared-from-strait-of-hormuz",
      "title": "Trump says all mines cleared from Strait of Hormuz",
      "summary": "Iran has closed the vital waterway since the beginning of the conflict causing wild fluctuations in oil prices worldwide.",
      "body": "US President Donald Trump has said that the US Navy has removed or detonated all mines in the Strait of Hormuz - the vital waterway which Iran has largely closed since the beginning of the war in the Middle East.\"Iran has been notified that any ship or boat placing new mines will be immediately and systematically destroyed,\" Trump said in a post on his Truth Social platform.There is no comment from Iran. Closure of the strait has caused wild fluctuations in the price of oil on world markets. Trump has often said the US has control of the area - but Iran has fired against vessels it says have sought to cross without its permission. Some 20% of the world's oil and liquefied natural gas used to transit the Strait of Hormuz before the war, which began on 28 February with US and Israeli air strikes against Iran.In addition to closing the strait, Iran retaliated against Israel and against US bases in the region as well as US Arab allies in the Gulf.A ceasefire aimed at facilitating talks to end the war has collapsed and there has been intermittent exchange of fire between the US and Iran. However, this week the US announced a raft of economic sanctions against Iran.US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent warned that any nation financially partnering with Iran would be isolated.Iran and the US signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) in June - a 14-point document which set a 60-day deadline to reach a deal on the Strait of Hormuz, before substantial negotiations on resolving the nuclear issue - allegations that Iran is building a nuclear weapon, which Tehran has consistently denied.However, that deadline has now expired and talks have collapsed leading to the new US sanctions that Trump believes will lead to the collapse of the Iranian regime.Iran has been under sanctions for most of the time since the founding of the Islamic Republic in 1979 and Economy Minister Ali Madanizadeh said Tehran was \"fully prepared\" for the wider sanctions, which he added would lead to \"another defeat\" for the US.Its economy, however, has suffered as a result of the war - and a blockade of Iranian ports that the US has been maintaining for weeks not to allow trade to continue. On Tuesday, China - the biggest buyer of Iranian oil - said it was firmly opposed to what it called the US's \"illegal unilateral sanctions\" and would take \"all necessary measures\" to safeguard its rights. The US announcement comes ahead of planned talks between Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping next month.Washington is likely to be wary of potential retaliation from Beijing, which processes the majority of the world's rare earths and other critical minerals, crucial in the manufacture of many high-tech products.Beijing has already tightened export controls on rare earths as part of previous trade negotiations with the US.Iran, for its part, has indicated that it wants to establish full control of the Strait of Hormuz - a view that is opposed by the US and most other countries which believe that the passage constitutes international waters and transit should remain unimpeded as it was before the war.Until recently - and on the basis of the MoU - Iran had been involved in negotiations with Oman, a US ally, to agree a mechanism for administering the strait. However, those talks have so far not produced an agreement and, last week, Trump even threatened to bomb its ally \"if it gets in the way\" of his administration's own talks with Iran over the issue.Omani Foreign Minister Badr Albusaidi said on Tuesday that he hoped his country and Iran would soon announce the establishment of a temporary shipping corridor in the strait after meeting his Iranian counterpart Abbas Araghchi in Tehran.He said in a statement he also hoped for \"practical arrangements\" to restore safe navigation, adding: \"Future management of the strait and a permanent solution will follow in due course.\"Araghchi confirmed the meeting in a statement, saying \"regional solutions\" were discussed in talks with representatives from Pakistan and Oman.He added that discussions covered the future management of the strait, as well as the framework for a new corridor and a joint mine-clearing operation.Speaking to state television news after the meeting Iran's Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabad said the entry route to the Gulf would be \"through Iranian waters and the exit route through Omani territorial waters, and ships will pass through Iranian waters on both routes\".The minister added that the south corridor of the waterway, which has been recommended to shipping firms by the US and is situated close to the coast of Oman, would be \"closed\" under the framework, and the meeting \"does not mean that the Strait will be opened from tomorrow\".He said \"no military vessels will be allowed to pass through the Strait of Hormuz, and only commercial vessels will be allowed to pass through it\".Gharibabad called the route \"temporary\", adding Iran and Oman will negotiate a new permanent route within a timeframe of 60 days to replace the previous maritime traffic plan.\"If Iran's requests are not granted, the Strait will be closed,\" he said.Additional reporting by Ghoncheh Habibiazad from BBC Persian",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-25T21:23:41.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/e344/live/b6975340-a0a3-11f1-84a9-276d1a8e5a22.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwyzw836d05o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
